        "addressCheck": "Glatter does NOT support any Hasidic institution at נווה שאנן 33 — and the research correctly says so. I confirmed directly from the provided Glatter index that no institution carries the street \"נווה שאנן\" or any Neve Sha'anan house number; every Glatter address is elsewhere (דיזנגוף 36 מודז'יץ, פיארברג 35 ויז'ניץ, רוטשילד 112 בוהוש, הלל הזקן 10 סטרטין, מוהליבר 37 ללוב, העבודה 15 מונקאטש, פנקס, אחד העם, רש\"י, ליליינבלום). Open-web sources (Madlan, Project-TLV, Yad2) confirm נווה שאנן 33 / ראש פינה 7 is a residential תמ\"א 38/3 demolition-and-rebuild project (~7 floors), NOT a synagogue. The address claim — \"no Hasidic institution here\" — is correct. No correction to the address is needed because there is no real institution to relocate.",
        "sourceQuality": "Good for a negative finding. The two load-bearing sources resolve and support the claim: (1) Project-TLV / Madlan confirm נווה שאנן 33 is a residential urban-renewal building, not a synagogue; (2) the amhasefer.wordpress.com comment by Israel Reichman genuinely documents a Gur \"Beit HaHasidim\" book-stamp reading \"Kishon 33 Tel Aviv,\" corroborating the research's point that the only \"33\" Hasidic link is on a DIFFERENT street (הקישון 33), not נווה שאנן. Wikipedia corroborates the 1921 Neve Sha'anan founding by Jews leaving Jaffa after the תרפ\"א riots. The parshan.co.il synagogue-survey page failed to return body text in my fetch (could not independently re-verify the exact list), but WebSearch corroborated that Neve Sha'anan-street synagogues (Damascus/בית יעקב לעולי דמשק, Georgian/הר סיני, פועלי צדק, בית מרמורש) are community/edot synagogues, none Hasidic. Sources are real-estate and community sites rather than academic, which is appropriate and sufficient for debunking a non-existent institution.",
        "dynastyCheck": "No dynasty is claimed (\"dynasty\":\"\") and none is defensible. Correct. No Hasidic court, shtibel, or beit midrash is attested at נווה שאנן 33 in either Glatter or the open web. The research's care in NOT inventing a dynasty is exactly right.",
        "founderCheck": "No founder claimed and none exists — correct. The field is empty, which is the only defensible value given there is no institution.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "No founding year claimed — correct. (The 1921 date in the narrative refers to the Neve Sha'anan NEIGHBORHOOD's establishment, accurately attributed, not to any institution at #33.)",
        "imageCheck": "No image supplied (\"images\":[]). Appropriate: with no identified rebbe/dynasty/institution there is no portrait to attach, and the research correctly declined to fabricate one. Nothing to falsify.",
        "corrections": "The debunking holds; no substantive correction needed. Minor caveats to flag: (1) Apartment count is inconsistent across sources — the research says ~21 units, but current Yad2/Madlan listings indicate ~32 units. Not material to the Hasidic question. (2) The research states the Kishon-33 Gur shtibel \"is not in the Glatter index\" — strictly true (no Kishon address appears in Glatter), but note that Glatter DOES list גור (Gur) as a dynasty/beit-midrash with ~16 prayer-houses in Tel Aviv (rebbe based in Jerusalem, archival ref אעת\"א חטיבה 8 מיכל 2331), just without a street/number. So \"Gur is absent from Glatter\" would be wrong; \"Kishon 33 is absent from Glatter\" is right — the research's wording is accurate. (3) The suggested next step (check whether the map editor meant הקישון 33 / Gur) is sound, but even Kishon 33 lacks a Glatter house-number entry, so it cannot be promoted to an authoritative Glatter match either. Bottom line: where Glatter and the research could diverge, they agree — Glatter has zero Neve Sha'anan entries, and the research correctly reports the address as a non-synagogue / probable community-map error with low confidence.",
