      "verdict": {
        "overallVerdict": "mostly-solid",
        "addressCheck": "PARTIAL. The address \"בר אילן 4, תל אביב\" for the synagogue \"בית יעקב ואוהל רבקה - חסידי גור\" is well-supported by OPEN-WEB sources but NOT by Glatter. Confirmed by: (1) the Tel Aviv Religious Council (rabanut.co.il) record — name \"בית יעקב ואוהל רבקה - חסידי גור\", address \"בר אילן 4, לב העיר\", nusach Ashkenaz, affiliation \"חסידי גור\"; (2) easy.co.il (403 on fetch but appears in search as Bar Ilan 4, Gur synagogue); (3) the ynet article, which I opened and which explicitly contains the string \"בית המדרש של חסידי גור, רחוב בר אילן 4\". HOWEVER, the AUTHORITATIVE Glatter index does NOT tie Gur to Bar Ilan 4 or to any house number — the Gur entry is type \"beit-midrash\" with only an archival ref (אעת\"א, חטיבה 8, מיכל 2331, תיק 30-1960-2) and a general statement of \"16 בתי תפילה\" in 1960, no street, no number. Glatter assigns specific house numbers to OTHER courts (Modzhitz/Dizengoff 36, Vizhnitz/Fierberg 35, Bohush/Rothschild 112, etc.) but deliberately leaves Gur as a network without a pinpoint address. The research JSON discloses this honestly in its \"gaps\" field. So: address is real and currently active, but the specific house number is an open-web/directory fact, NOT a Glatter-validated one. No conflict (Glatter is silent, not contradictory), but the claim's specificity exceeds what the academic source supports.",
        "sourceQuality": "Good and mostly honest. All four cited URLs resolve and support their claims: ynet (opened — verbatim contains \"בית המדרש של חסידי גור, רחוב בר אילן 4\" and \"no fewer than 20 Gur synagogues mid-century\"); rabanut.co.il (opened — confirms name/address/Ashkenaz/Gur); easy.co.il (403 on direct fetch but corroborated via search); Time Out (opened — confirms Gur institutions on Bar Ilan, Fierberg, Yavne streets, and cites Weinstock that there were \"14 synagogues identified only with Gur in the 1960s, today three remain\"). The Glatter PDF (in.bgu.ac.il/bgi/iyunim/36/36-Michal-Glatter.pdf) is the real underlying academic source and was located, though its server refused direct fetch (ECONNREFUSED/403); its Gur content was confirmed via search snippets. One inconsistency: three different synagogue counts circulate — Glatter \"16\" (1960), ynet \"~20\", Time Out/Weinstock \"14\" — and the research's significance field hedges to \"16-20\" while ignoring the \"14\" it itself cites. Not fabrication, but loose.",
        "corrections": "1) Address/number caveat: state explicitly that \"Bar Ilan 4\" comes from the Tel Aviv Religious Council directory + ynet, NOT from Glatter; Glatter does not assign Gur a house number. The \"confidence: high\" should be downgraded to \"medium\" for the address-to-period linkage, since the SPECIFIC Bar Ilan 4 building cannot be confirmed as one of the 1960-era 16 prayer houses — it may be a later survivor. 2) Synagogue count: harmonize the figures honestly — Glatter=16 (1960), ynet=~20, Time Out/Weinstock=14. The \"כ-16-20\" range is defensible; but the story's flat \"כ-20\" leaning on ynet should also acknowledge the 14/16 academic figures. 3) foundingYear \"unknown\" is the correct, honest call — neither Glatter nor any web source dates the Bar Ilan 4 beit midrash; do not invent one. 4) The \"early settlement before WWII / 1920s rebbe encouragement\" framing is accurate (Gur aliyah 1919-1929, Shem MiShmuel/Imrei Emet encouragement) and corroborated. 5) Image: the research's decision to include NO image is CORRECT — File:Beis_Yisroel.jpg shows three men (not a clean single portrait), is dated 2007 (post-dates Alter's 1977 death, so it is a derivative/uploaded scan with dubious historical licensing). Do not use it as a portrait. 6) Minor: Rebbe Yisrael Alter is correctly framed as Jerusalem-based dynasty head (1948-1977), NOT a resident of Bar Ilan 4 — the JSON states this correctly; keep that disclaimer.",
        "founderCheck": "SOLID with correct framing. Two-layer founder claim, both verified: (a) Dynasty founder Rabbi Yitzchak Meir Alter (\"Chiddushei HaRim\", 1799-1866), founded Ger in Góra Kalwaria c.1859 after the Kotzker Rebbe's death — confirmed (Wikipedia/OU/JVL). (b) Local Tel Aviv representative Yehuda Meir Abramowicz — REAL: b.1914 Lodz, immigrated 1935, settled in Tel Aviv, deputy mayor of Tel Aviv-Yafo 1955-1971, Knesset member representing Agudat Israel/Gur, led daily Torah classes in Tel Aviv. His tie to the Gur Tel Aviv community is genuine. NEITHER is claimed to have founded THIS specific synagogue (no source names the Bar Ilan 4 founder), and the JSON correctly does not assert that — it presents them as dynasty founder and local communal figure, which is accurate framing rather than fabrication.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "HONEST. foundingYear is \"unknown (mid-20th c.; Gur active in TA from 1920s-30s, network peaked ~1960).\" No source — Glatter, ynet, rabanut, or Time Out — dates the Bar Ilan 4 beit midrash specifically. The contextual dates given (Gur aliyah 1919-1929; peak ~1960) are accurate for the dynasty's TA presence broadly. The research correctly refuses to fabricate a building-specific year. Defensible.",
        "dynastyCheck": "SOLID. Gur (גור / Ger) attribution is confirmed by BOTH the open web (rabanut directory lists \"חסידי גור\" in the official name; easy.co.il; kipa) AND the Glatter index (which has a dedicated גור/Gur beit-midrash entry, calling Gur \"the largest Hasidic nucleus, reaching 16 prayer houses in early 1960\"). The community map left the dynasty field blank and the research filled it as Gur on solid grounds. No conflation with another court. Note: the JSON also correctly distinguishes the Tel Aviv beit-midrash network from the main Gur beit midrash in Jerusalem (Jeremiah St.) — no confusion there.",
        "imageCheck": "CORRECTLY OMITTED. The only candidate, File:Beis_Yisroel.jpg (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0), depicts THREE religious figures (Yisrael Alter flanked by R. Yaakov Weil and the Tolna Rebbe per the file's data) — not a clean single portrait — and carries a 2007 upload date though the subject died in 1977, making its historical license provenance questionable. It is a photograph (not a gravestone, not a fabricated URL), but unsuitable as a portrait for this institution. The research's cautious decision to include NO image is the right call. No fabricated image URL was asserted.",
        "flaggedClaims": [
          "Address specificity overreach: 'Bar Ilan 4' is presented with 'confidence: high' but rests ONLY on the rabanut directory + ynet, NOT on Glatter; Glatter assigns Gur no house number. The building cannot be confirmed as one of the 1960-era prayer houses.",
          "Conflicting synagogue counts: significance says '~16-20', story says '~20' (ynet), but the cited Time Out source says '14' (Weinstock) and Glatter says '16' — the research does not reconcile these and leans on the highest figure.",
          "Mild anachronism risk in story: framing the dynasty leadership period (Yisrael Alter 1948-1977) alongside the Bar Ilan 4 building implies a temporal link that no source establishes; JSON mitigates this with a disclaimer, so it is borderline not a hard error.",
          "Image metadata: File:Beis_Yisroel.jpg dated 2007 despite subject's 1977 death — derivative scan with dubious license history (correctly excluded)."
