      "verdict": {
        "overallVerdict": "mostly-solid",
        "addressCheck": "The mapped address (לאן א' 5) is a real location — \"Simta Lean\" is a genuine pair of alleys (לאן א'/לאן ב') branching off Feierberg St. in central Tel Aviv (confirmed: project-tlv.info lists \"Feierberg 23 / Simta Lean A 1\"). BUT Glatter does NOT support tying Ozarov to this address. Glatter's Ozarov entry is type \"rebbe-home\" with NO street, NO house number, and NO archival file reference — just \"רבי יחיאל משה אפשטיין ... אוז'רוב ... ניו יורק ... 1953\". So Glatter cannot and does not place Ozarov at Lean/Feierberg. Independently, both Hebrew Wikipedia articles place his documented synagogue at Rothschild Blvd / Lunz St corner, near the Bilu School, and his residence on Rothschild Blvd until his 1971 death — a different part of central Tel Aviv. The research's correctedAddress (Rothschild/Lunz) is the one supported by sources. Glatter, where it overlaps, does NOT override this; it simply provides no address at all for Ozarov, so it neither confirms Lean nor contradicts Rothschild. The research's mapErrorFlag \"unverifiable\" is the correct, honest verdict.",
        "sourceQuality": "Strong and largely accurate. The cited Wikipedia articles (משה יחיאל הלוי אפשטיין; יהדות אורתודוקסית בתל אביב; חסידות אוז'רוב) resolve and genuinely support the Rothschild/Lunz synagogue location and the rebbe's biography. The Feierberg/Lean etymology (named after Mordechai Ze'ev Feierberg's novella \"לאן?\", answer = Eretz Israel) is independently confirmed via the Feierberg Wikipedia entry and project-tlv. mytzadik.com (id=593) corroborates 1953 aliyah (י\"ז תמוז תשי\"ג) and Bnei Brak burial. Sources are real, on-point, and the research does not overstate them — it explicitly admits NO source links Ozarov to Lean/Feierberg.",
        "corrections": "1) Map attribution is unsupported/likely an error: no source (Glatter or web) places Ozarov chasidut at לאן א' 5 or anywhere on Feierberg. The Feierberg-area shtiebels that ARE documented are GUR (Feierberg 17, per research) and VIZHNITZ (Feierberg 35) — the latter confirmed by Glatter with archival ref את\"ב תיק 650130, מסמכים 7127528/7127291. A conflation between these and Ozarov is plausible. Correct documented Ozarov location: Rothschild Blvd / Lunz St corner. 2) Birth year: research says 1889 (correct; ה'תר\"ן = 1889/90); note mytzadik.com erroneously glosses ה'תר\"ן as \"1879\" — disregard that. 3) Name order: research uses משה יחיאל (Moshe Yechiel), which matches Wikipedia and mytzadik; Glatter's \"יחיאל משה\" is reversed/erroneous — the research correctly flags this. 4) Aliyah year: research/Glatter/mytzadik = 1953; Hebrew Wikipedia's biographical infobox says 1954 — minor source discrepancy worth noting, but 1953 (י\"ז תמוז תשי\"ג) is the better-attested date. 5) No portrait image was attached, which is the right call — no free-licensed portrait was found and none should be fabricated.",
        "founderCheck": "Real and correctly identified. Rabbi Moshe Yechiel Halevi Epstein (1889–1971), Ozarov-Chęciny (חנצ'ין) rebbe, author of \"אש דת\" (11 vols) and \"באר משה\", Israel Prize for Torah literature 1968 (and Rabbi Kook Prize 1959), member of Moetzet Gedolei HaTorah. He was the Ozarov/Chantshin rebbe via maternal descent from R' Chaim Shmuel of Chantshin. All confirmed by Wikipedia and mytzadik. HOWEVER: he is the founder of the dynasty's TA presence generally, NOT specifically tied to THIS address. His documented synagogue was at Rothschild/Lunz, not Lean/Feierberg.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "1953 is defensible and well-attested (research, Glatter, and mytzadik all agree: י\"ז תמוז ה'תשי\"ג = 1953 aliyah from New York). Caveat: Hebrew Wikipedia's bio gives the immigration date as 1954 — a real minor discrepancy. The \"founding of the synagogue at Rothschild-Lunz\" framing is reasonable but the synagogue founding year itself is not precisely sourced; 1953 properly refers to the aliyah. As applied to the Lean address it is moot, since the address attribution itself is unsupported.",
        "dynastyCheck": "Dynasty attribution (Ozarov / אוז'רוב, aka Ozarov-Chęciny / אוזרוב-חנצ'ין) is correct and well-described: origin in Ozarów, Poland; most chasidim perished in the Holocaust; center today in Bnei Brak (where the rebbe is buried, on Chazon Ish St). The dynastyBackground is accurate. The ONLY problem is geographic: the dynasty is real but not tied to the Lean/Feierberg address.",
        "imageCheck": "No image was included in the claim. This is correct and honest — the research explicitly states no free-licensed portrait exists on Wikimedia Commons (the he-wiki image is a non-free local upload / group photo), so none was fabricated. No gravestone or bogus URL was substituted. Pass.",
        "flaggedClaims": [
          "Map attribution 'Ozarov shtiebel at Lean Alef 5' — UNSUPPORTED by any source; research itself flags it 'unverifiable'. Likely conflation with documented Feierberg shtiebels of Gur (17) and Vizhnitz (35).",
          "Glatter's name order 'יחיאל משה אפשטיין' is reversed; correct is משה יחיאל (research notes this).",
          "Aliyah year: research/Glatter/mytzadik say 1953; Hebrew Wikipedia bio says 1954 — minor unresolved discrepancy.",
          "mytzadik.com glosses birth year ה'תר\"ן as '1879' — that is an arithmetic error; correct is 1889 as the research states.",
          "Glatter provides NO street/number/archival ref for Ozarov, so it cannot corroborate ANY specific Tel Aviv address for this institution — including the corrected Rothschild/Lunz one (which rests on Wikipedia, not Glatter)."
        ]
      }
