        "sourceQuality": "Mixed but adequate. Both Hebrew Wikipedia articles (Hasidut Otynia; Chaim Hager) resolve and substantiate the dynasty, founder, dates, successor, and books. The Glatter PDF (in.bgu.ac.il/.../36-Michal-Glatter.pdf) is the load-bearing academic source for the address but would NOT load via direct fetch (ECONNREFUSED, repeatedly -- the same failure the original research reported), so it was verifiable only through a Google search-result excerpt, not full text; that excerpt DOES match the quoted claim, the 1943 letter, and the archival reference. The ynet article resolves and confirms Otynia was a showcased Tel Aviv court but gives no address. The rabanut 'Rashi-2' deep link 404s. The 5th-listed OSU PDF is unrelated (a Hebrew lexicon, NOT Glatter) -- if the JSON intended it as a Glatter mirror it is mis-cited, but the JSON sources list points to the ynet/rabanut/Wikipedia set, so this is only a side observation. Wikimedia Commons MediaSearch returns no free portrait of R. Chaim Hager.",
        "founderCheck": "Founder is REAL and correctly identified. Rabbi Chaim Hager of Otynia (1862-1931), second son of Rabbi Baruch Hager (2nd Admor of Vizhnitz), founded the court in the town of Otynia/Otinia in Eastern Galicia -- confirmed by both Wikipedia articles. His books Tal Chaim (on Torah) and Nimukei Chaim (on Shas) are confirmed. IMPORTANT distinction the JSON handles correctly: Chaim Hager founded the DYNASTY in Galicia; he is NOT the founder of the Tel Aviv shtibel (which Holocaust survivors established later). So the founder is tied to the dynasty, not specifically to the Tel Aviv address -- the JSON states this accurately.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "Defensible with one internal slip. The Tel Aviv shtibel operated by at least summer 1943 (the dated gabbaim letter) -- well supported. The dynasty's Galician founding is given as 'late 19th century'; Wikipedia is internally inconsistent (intro says late 19th c.; body ties the founding to the death of R. Baruch in taf-resh-nun-gimel). NOTE the gematria taf-resh-nun-gimel = 1893, not 1892 -- and one Wikipedia rendering even mis-translates it as 1883. The research JSON's dynastyBackground says Chaim acquired the Otynia land 'after his father's death' which is correct in spirit, though it elsewhere implies a vaguer 'late 19th century'. Not a fabrication, just dating imprecision inherited from the sources.",
        "dynastyCheck": "Dynasty attribution HOLDS. Otynia (also spelled Atina/Antania) is a real Vizhnitz offshoot court from Eastern Galicia; the Glatter index itself labels the shtibel 'Vizhnitz-Otynia', and separately lists a bare 'Otynia (Atina)' shtibel among Tel Aviv courts. Both Glatter and the open web agree on the dynasty. The map label 'Otnia' maps cleanly to this court.",
        "imageCheck": "No image was attached, which is CORRECT. The only image of R. Chaim Hager on Hebrew Wikipedia is a GRAVESTONE photo (his kever in Stanislav), NOT a portrait. Wikimedia Commons MediaSearch returns no free-licensed portrait. The JSON correctly declined to attach a portrait and correctly flagged this gap. Had a portrait been claimed, it would have been a fabrication; it was not.",
