      "verdict": {
        "overallVerdict": "mostly-solid",
        "addressCheck": "Glatter does NOT support \"Geula 32\". Both the Glatter index and the underlying BGU academic article (Michal Glatter, \"Admorim in the Streets of Tel Aviv 1940-1965\") place the Vizhnitz-Otynia (ויז'ניץ-אטינא) shtibel on Rashi street (רחוב רש\"י), tied to a summer-1943 letter from the synagogue gabbai to the Tel Aviv municipality requesting the \"Achad Ha'am\" school for High Holiday prayers, archival ref אעת\"א חטיבה 8 מיכל 1084 תיק 2288. \"Otynia (אטינה)\" otherwise appears only in Glatter's generic list of \"other Hasidic centers in the city\" with no street and no number. Glatter never links Otynia to Geula or to house number 32. So the map pin (Geula 32) is unsupported, and the research's corrected location (Rashi street) is correct and Glatter-backed. Caveat: Glatter gives NO house number for the Rashi shtibel, so \"רחוב רש\"י\" is the most precise defensible address — the research correctly does not invent a number.",
        "sourceQuality": "Mixed-to-good. Dynasty/founder claims are fully corroborated by both cited Hebrew Wikipedia articles (חסידות אוטיניא and חיים הגר (אוטיניה)), which resolve and confirm lineage, dates, and book titles. The Glatter BGU PDF (in.bgu.ac.il/bgi/iyunim/36/36-Michal-Glatter.pdf) is a genuine peer-reviewed academic source and independently corroborates the Rashi-street/1943/archival-ref claim via web-search snippets (the PDF host repeatedly returned ECONNREFUSED on direct fetch, but the content was confirmed indirectly). WEAK SPOT: the Geula-32 \"Russian consulate/embassy school (посольская школа)\" detail is NOT supported by the cited Project-TLV or VTLV pages — Project-TLV only lists \"פרויקט גאולה 32\" (a modern real-estate development), and the VTLV page is merely a contact form. No fabricated URLs in the sources array; all listed sources are real, though two are over-cited for a claim they don't actually make.",
        "corrections": "1) ADDRESS: Use \"רחוב רש\"י, תל אביב\" (no documented house number) instead of Geula 32; the research already does this correctly. Do NOT assert any specific number on Rashi. 2) SUCCESSION ERROR: The research says son \"Israel Shalom Yosef Hager inherited him but was murdered in 1944.\" Wikipedia shows son Menachem Mendel succeeded FIRST (died young), THEN Israel Shalom Yosef (\"Nefesh Yesharah\") led and was murdered in Iyar 5704 / May 1944 with his children. The research skips Menachem Mendel — minor but real conflation. 3) POST-HOLOCAUST SUCCESSOR: Research credits \"Rabbi Moshe Hager\" with founding yeshivat \"Tal Chaim\" in 1969 and being the Otynia successor. Wikipedia confirms Rabbi Moshe (grandson of R. Yechiel Michel Hager) was crowned Otynia Rebbe after the Holocaust by R. Chaim Meir of Vizhnitz; the Tal Chaim yeshiva detail is plausible but not directly verified in the sources I could open — flag as low-confidence. 4) DELETE the \"Russian consulate/embassy school\" claim for Geula 32 as unsupported. The accurate, source-backed statement is simply: Geula 32 is in Kerem HaTeimanim with no documented Hasidic shtibel; Geula 30 housed the secular Gymnasia Geula (1919–1979). The Russian Embassy was at Rothschild 46, not Geula. 5) FOUNDING YEAR: Wikipedia says land purchased in 1893 (תרנ\"ג); research's \"~1892\" (year of father's death) is close and acceptable but 1893 is more precise.",
        "founderCheck": "CONFIRMED REAL and correctly described as the DYNASTY founder, but NOT tied to this address. Rabbi Chaim Hager of Otynia (1862–1931), son of Baruch Hager (2nd Rebbe of Vizhnitz) and grandson of Menachem Mendel Hager the \"Tzemach Tzedek,\" founder of Vizhnitz — all verified on both Wikipedia articles. His books \"Tal Chaim\" (on Torah) and \"Nimukei Chaim\" (on Shas) are confirmed. He founded the court in Otynia, Galicia — he has NO connection to Tel Aviv or to Geula 32; the research is appropriately careful to separate the Galician founder from the Tel Aviv shtibel.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "Defensible. Court founded ~1892–1893 (Wikipedia: land purchased תרנ\"ג/1893 after father's death in 1892). The Tel Aviv shtibel is documented in 1943 (Glatter). The research correctly marks the Geula-32 founding year as \"unknown\" since the address itself is unsupported. No anachronism in the dating.",
        "dynastyCheck": "HOLDS. Otynia (Otiniya / Vizhnitz-Atina) is correctly identified as a pre-Holocaust East Galician branch of Vizhnitz Hasidism. Glatter independently lists both \"ויז'ניץ-אטינא\" (Rashi st. shtibel) and \"אטינה\" as Hasidic centers. The Vizhnitz family (Hager) lineage is consistent across all sources. One nuance the research omits: after WWI the court relocated to Stanislavov — not contradictory, just incomplete.",
        "imageCheck": "Clean. The images array is empty. The research honestly documents that it could not open a valid free-license page for a portrait of R. Chaim Hager on Wikimedia (404) and therefore attached none. No fabricated image URL, no gravestone, no misrepresented portrait. This is the correct conservative behavior.",
