      "verdict": {
        "overallVerdict": "likely-fabricated",
        "addressCheck": "NO. Neither Glatter nor the open web supports an Amshinov shtibel at Berdichevsky 3, Tel Aviv. (1) The Glatter Amshinov entry has NO street, NO house number, and NO archivalRef field — confirmed by direct inspection of the index; it lists only dynasty=Amshinov, rebbe=Yerachmiel Yehuda Meir Kalish, origin=USA, year=1946, type=rebbe-home. By contrast, entries Glatter DOES geolocate (Modzhitz/Dizengoff 36, Munkacs/HaAvoda 15, Vizhnitz/Piarberg 35, Stratyn/Hillel HaZaken 10, Bohush/Rothschild 112, Lelov/Mohliver 37) all carry an archivalRef with a Tel Aviv Archive file number. Amshinov has none, so Glatter cannot 'override' to Berdichevsky 3 — it is silent on location. (2) project-tlv.info confirms Berdichevsky 3-5 = 'Beit Agudat Israel.' (3) Decisive new evidence: the institution actually documented at Berdichevsky 3-5 is the Novardok 'Beit Yosef' yeshiva (founded 1929 by R. Hillel Witkind, moved to Berdichevsky 3-5; building committee address given as Berdichevsky 3-5) — a Lithuanian MITNAGED yeshiva, the ideological opposite of a hasidic Amshinov shtibel. So the address is not merely unsupported; it is occupied by a documented non-hasidic institution. correctedAddress is unknown — no defensible Amshinov address can be asserted.",
        "sourceQuality": "Mixed but mostly serviceable. He.wikipedia (Yerachmiel Yehuda Meir Kalish) RESOLVES and supports the biography: b.1905 Pshischa, d.1976 Jerusalem; father R. Shimon Shalom Kalish; fled to Vilna→Shanghai; moved to USA late 1946; came to Israel only after father's death in 1954, settled briefly in Tel Aviv, founded Shem Olam yeshiva in Holon, moved to Jerusalem/Beit Vegan Dec 1955. He.wikipedia (Amshinov) RESOLVES and confirms 1848 founding by R. Yaakov David Kalish in Mszczonów and an Amshinov shtibel 'Ohel Yosef' in Tel Aviv (named for R. Yosef Kalish, d.1936) BUT gives NO address. project-tlv.info RESOLVES and supports Beit Agudat Israel at Berdichevsky 3-5. WEAK/MISLEADING SOURCE: rabanut.co.il 'Ohel Yosef – Beit Zacharia' — the page describes a YEMENITE (Temani) synagogue with NO Amshinov connection stated, and the live page shows Sderot HaChayal 33, while Waze/search index show 30. So the research's own equation of Amshinov's 'Ohel Yosef' with this HaChayal synagogue is itself an unverified conflation (the historic Amshinov Ohel Yosef has no confirmed surviving address). Commons category check is honest — only file is of the father, R. Shimon Shalom Kalish, not the subject.",
        "founderCheck": "Founder figures are REAL and correctly named. Dynasty founder R. Yaakov David Kalish (Mszczonów/Amshinov, ~1848), son of R. Yitzchak of Vorka — confirmed by Wikipedia. The local rebbe R. Yerachmiel Yehuda Meir Kalish (1905-1976), 'Reb Meir'l of Amshinov,' son of R. Shimon Shalom Kalish of Amshinov-Otwock — confirmed. PROBLEM: he is NOT tied to Berdichevsky 3. In 1946 (the Glatter year) he was in transit Shanghai→USA, not in Tel Aviv; he reached Israel only ~1954 and his Tel Aviv stay was brief and transient before Holon and then Jerusalem. There is no documented institution founded by him at this address.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "1946 is INDEFENSIBLE as a Tel Aviv founding year. In Glatter, '1946' is the rebbe's ORIGIN year/location = USA (he was abroad), explicitly a rebbe-home origin marker, not a Tel Aviv shtibel founding. The map/claim's reading of 1946 as a Berdichevsky-3 founding is a category error: the rebbe physically arrived in Israel only ~1954. The research JSON itself flags this correctly. No founding year for any Amshinov institution at Berdichevsky 3 can be established.",
        "dynastyCheck": "Dynasty label 'Amshinov' is correct in the abstract — it is a genuine, important hasidic dynasty (Vorka/Pshischa-Kotzk lineage). BUT the dynasty-to-address attribution fails: the documented Amshinov presence in Tel Aviv is the 'Ohel Yosef' shtibel (no confirmed surviving address), while Berdichevsky 3-5 is documented as Beit Agudat Israel housing the Novardok (Lithuanian, anti-hasidic) Beit Yosef yeshiva. Attaching Amshinov to Berdichevsky 3 conflates a real dynasty with the wrong building.",
        "imageCheck": "No image present, and correctly so. The research notes — and Commons Category:Amshinov confirms — that no free-licensed portrait of R. Yerachmiel Yehuda Meir Kalish exists there; the only relevant file depicts his FATHER R. Shimon Shalom Kalish (a different person). Omitting the image was the right call; using the father's photo would have been a misattribution. No fabricated image URL detected.",
        "flaggedClaims": [
          "Address 'Berdichevsky 3, Tel Aviv' for an Amshinov shtibel — UNSUPPORTED by Glatter (no street/number/archivalRef) and contradicted on the ground (Beit Agudat Israel; Novardok Beit Yosef yeshiva there).",
          "Year 1946 misread as a Tel Aviv founding — it is the rebbe's USA origin year in Glatter; he was not in Israel until ~1954 (ANACHRONISM).",
          "Equation of Amshinov 'Ohel Yosef' with the synagogue at Sderot HaChayal 30/33 — the rabbanut page describes a YEMENITE synagogue with no Amshinov link; this is an unverified conflation in the research itself (address also inconsistent: 30 vs 33).",
          "Implicit claim that R. Yerachmiel Yehuda Meir Kalish established/led a Tel Aviv institution — he only stayed in Tel Aviv transiently post-1954 before Holon and Jerusalem.",
          "Speculation that an Amshinov shtibel operated inside Beit Agudat Israel at Berdichevsky 3 — explicitly undocumented; the building's documented occupant is a Lithuanian/mitnaged yeshiva."
        ],
        "corrections": "1) Map attribution 'Amshinov shtibel at Berdichevsky 3' should be marked UNVERIFIABLE/likely erroneous. Glatter does not place Amshinov at any address — it gives no street, number, or archive file for Amshinov (unlike the six dynasties it DOES geolocate with archivalRef). 2) The 1946 datum is the rebbe's USA origin year, not a Tel Aviv founding; do not present 1946 as an institution founding date. 3) Berdichevsky 3-5 is documented as 'Beit Agudat Israel' and as the home of the Novardok 'Beit Yosef' yeshiva (R. Hillel Witkind, est. 1929, cornerstone May 1942) — a Lithuanian mitnaged institution, not hasidic and not Amshinov. 4) The only documented Amshinov presence in Tel Aviv is the 'Ohel Yosef' shtibel (named for R. Yosef Kalish, d.1936); its address is NOT confirmed — the research's link to Sderot HaChayal 30/33 rests on a rabbanut page that actually describes a Yemenite synagogue, so that 'corrected address' should also be treated as unverified, not asserted. 5) Founder and dynasty names are accurate and may be retained, but must be decoupled from the Berdichevsky 3 location. 6) Keep image omitted; do not substitute the father's portrait. Net: where Glatter and the open web are compared, both AGREE there is no Amshinov-at-Berdichevsky-3 support; Glatter is 'correct' only in the narrow sense that it never made the address claim — the address appears to originate from the map, not from Glatter."
