      "verdict": {
        "overallVerdict": "mostly-solid",
        "addressCheck": "PARTIALLY. Glatter does NOT support Balfour 44 — it contains no entry for that address or for any \"Ashlag\" institution there, and the claim honestly admits this. The address is instead supported by the open-web heritage source (Golden Lotus / golden-lotus.co.il/shevach), which explicitly states Baal HaSulam lived in the Beit Shevach building at Balfour 44 (corner Rothschild 88) and that his son Shlomo Binyamin Ashlag ran the \"Beit Yehuda\" study hall in his apartment there. So the address-to-person link is genuinely sourced (not merely the dynasty elsewhere), but it rests entirely on a local heritage website, not an archival file number. No fabricated Glatter house number was imported — good. Glatter's nearest record (Stratyn-Ashlag / Brandwein shtibel at Hillel HaZaken 10, file 1200100) is correctly kept SEPARATE; the claim does not conflate the two, which is correct.",
        "sourceQuality": "Mixed. The PERSON (Ashlag) and the building (Beit Shevach, architect Moshe Tzerner/משה צ'רנר, built 1926) are solidly documented across Hebrew/English Wikipedia and the Golden Lotus heritage page, which all resolve and support the claims. BUT the specific assertion that Ashlag lived at Balfour 44 and that \"Beit Yehuda\" operated there rests on a SINGLE non-academic heritage website (Golden Lotus) plus a Facebook page — no archival file number exists for this address, exactly as the claim's own 'gaps' field concedes. The Glatter index is internally consistent and does NOT contradict the address (it simply has no entry). The image source (Wikimedia Commons File:YehudaAshlag.png) resolves and is a genuine public-domain youth portrait photograph (PD-Mark), not a gravestone or fabricated URL.",
        "corrections": "1) YEAR — biggest error. The claim's foundingYear/story/significance state Ashlag lived in the Balfour 44 building \"1943–1954.\" This CONFLATES two distinct moves: 1943 is when he moved to Tel Aviv generally. Sources (mudaut.co.il, kab.co.il chronology) show he first lived at YAVNE 4, and only moved to BALFOUR 44 in 1951 (תשי\"א), dying there in 1954. Correct residence span at this address: 1951–1954, NOT 1943–1954. 2) BIRTHPLACE — dynastyBackground says Ashlag was \"born in Warsaw\"; he was born in ŁUKÓW (Lukow), Congress Poland (he later lived/served as rabbi in Warsaw). 3) IMMIGRATION — \"עלה לארץ ישראל ב-1921\" is roughly right (emigrated 1921 per EN-wiki, in the Holy Land from 1922); but the background omits his Givat Shaul rabbinate (1924) and Jaffa period (1932) and overstates a continuous Jerusalem-then-Tel-Aviv arc. 4) HASIDIC TIES — \"פורסוב, בלז וגור\" — Porisov and Belz are corroborated; GUR is NOT supported by the cited Wikipedia and should be flagged as unverified. 5) BUILD YEAR — story gives \"1926–1929 (מצוין 1926–1929)\"; the heritage source unambiguously dates it to 1926 (תרפ\"ו, embedded in the cornice). The \"1929\" alternate is unsupported and should be dropped. 6) currentStatus 'closed' and confidence 'medium' are appropriate. Net: real founder, real building, real address link — but fix the 1951 (not 1943) residence date, the Łuków birthplace, the unverified Gur tie, and the spurious 1929 build date.",
        "dynastyCheck": "Defensible with a caveat. Ashlag IS the founder of the Ashlag (Baal HaSulam) Kabbalistic-Hasidic school, and the claim correctly notes this is an esoteric/Kabbalistic school rather than a classical Hasidic court with a continuous Admor dynasty — an honest and accurate characterization. His family's ties to Porisov and Belz are confirmed; the additional \"Gur\" tie is NOT corroborated by the cited sources and should be flagged. The successors named (sons Baruch Shalom Ashlag and Shlomo Binyamin Ashlag; brother-in-law/student Yehuda Tzvi Brandwein of Stratyn) are all real and correctly described.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "FLAG — conflation. The claim repeatedly frames Ashlag's Balfour 44 residence as 1943–1954. The 1943 date is his general move to Tel Aviv; the SPECIFIC Balfour 44 residence began only in 1951 (תשי\"א) after an intervening residence at Yavne 4 (from 1946). He died at Balfour 44 in 1954. The \"Beit Yehuda\" study hall run by his son post-1954 is plausibly dated. Correct the residence span to 1951–1954.",
        "founderCheck": "Real and specifically tied to THIS address. Rabbi Yehuda Leib Halevi Ashlag (Baal HaSulam, 1885–1954) is a major, well-documented Kabbalist. The Golden Lotus heritage page explicitly names him as a tenant of Beit Shevach at Balfour 44 and identifies his son Shlomo Binyamin Ashlag as running the \"Beit Yehuda\" study hall in the apartment. So this is NOT a case of attaching a dynasty that was actually located elsewhere — the person is genuinely linked to this specific building by the available source (a heritage website, not an archive).",
        "imageCheck": "VERIFIED. https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a1/YehudaAshlag.png is a genuine, resolving Wikimedia Commons file: a real public-domain photographic PORTRAIT of Yehuda Ashlag (in his youth), sourced originally from Bnei Baruch Kabbalah, carrying a Creative Commons Public Domain Mark (PD-Mark). It is NOT a gravestone and NOT a fabricated URL. License and caption attribution are accurate.",
        "flaggedClaims": [
