        "addressCheck": "The pinned address (Yehuda Halevi 85) is correctly flagged as UNVERIFIABLE/wrong for a Gur institution. Neither Glatter nor any open-web source ties Gur to Yehuda Halevi. Glatter's Gur entry (אעת\"א, חטיבה 8, מיכל 2331, תיק 30-1960-2) gives NO street or house number at all — it only describes Gur as \"the largest Hasidic nucleus, reaching 16 prayer houses in early תש\"ך (1960).\" So Glatter neither supports nor contradicts a specific Gur address; it simply has none, which the research states accurately. The research's corrected addresses are independently confirmed: rabanut.co.il lists the Gur synagogue at Fiarbarg (פיירברג) 17; Hebrew Wikipedia confirms \"Shtibl Echad Ha'am\" and \"Shtibl Kishon 33\"; web search confirms Gur congregations on Bar-Ilan and Fiarbarg. Yehuda Halevi 85 today is the commercial \"Beit Silon\" building housing the myki clothing store (confirmed via dapei zahav/easy.co.il) — not a synagogue. Verdict: the map pin is a genuine location error; corrected addresses hold.",
        "sourceQuality": "Good. All five cited URLs resolve and support their claims. rabanut.co.il confirms Fiarbarg 17. Hebrew Wikipedia (חסידות גור) confirms Echad Ha'am and Kishon 33 shtibels, the 1920s founding, the move to Bnei Brak, and Kiryat HaRi\"m Levin in the late 1970s. en.wikipedia/Yisrael_Alter confirms the Beis Yisroel led Ger 1948–1977, based in Jerusalem (Geulah), buried on Mount of Olives. The two business-directory links (dapei zahav, easy.co.il) confirm myki/Beit Silon at Yehuda Halevi 85. Glatter is used correctly as a constraint rather than over-read. Local representative Yehuda Meir Abramovich independently verified (Wikipedia/Haaretz obituary): Agudat Yisrael MK representing Gur and deputy mayor of Tel Aviv 1955–1971 — a fully plausible 1960 local figure.",
        "dynastyCheck": "Holds. Ger/Gur is correctly described as one of the largest Polish Hasidic courts, founded in Góra Kalwaria (Ger) near Warsaw by Yitzchak Meir Alter. The Tel Aviv community as the city's largest Hasidic nucleus matches both Glatter and Wikipedia.",
        "founderCheck": "Verified real; see founderCheck field above.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "Defensible. The research lists year as \"unknown for this address\" and notes the Tel Aviv community operated from the 1920s with 16 prayer houses by 1960. Minor source tension worth noting: Hebrew Wikipedia says \"7 synagogues by 1949,\" while Glatter says \"16 prayer houses in early 1960.\" These are not contradictory (growth over a decade) and the research cites the 16/1960 figure to Glatter. No anachronism in the founding-year framing.",
        "imageCheck": "Correctly handled. File:GerrerRebbe.JPG (Wikimedia Commons) is verified as a public-domain PORTRAIT — not a gravestone — but it depicts Yaakov Aryeh Alter (the current Gerrer Rebbe), photographed 2006, NOT Yisrael Alter who led Ger in 1960. The research correctly OMITTED it to avoid attaching a post-2000 image to a pre-1980 institution. This is the right call; using it would have been an anachronism.",
        "flaggedClaims": [
          "MAP PIN ERROR (primary): 'בית כנסת/שטיבל גור, יהודה הלוי 85' is unsupported by Glatter or any web source; Yehuda Halevi 85 is the commercial Beit Silon building (myki store), never a Gur synagogue. Correctly flagged as unverifiable.",
          "Glatter has NO address for Gur at all — any specific house number attributed to Gur via Glatter would be fabrication. The research correctly states Glatter gives no street/number.",
          "Potential source tension: Wikipedia '7 synagogues by 1949' vs Glatter '16 prayer houses 1960' — reconcilable as growth over time, not a contradiction; research attributes the 16 figure to Glatter correctly.",
