        "addressCheck": "NO — neither Glatter nor any open-web source supports \"גרוזנברג 14\". The Glatter index entry for גור (Gur) is a generic type:\"beit-midrash\" with NO street and NO number (archivalRef אעת\"א חטיבה 8 מיכל 2331 תיק 30-1960-2), and its quote only describes \"16 בתי תפילה בראשית תש\"ך (1960)\" scattered across the city. The forum source the claim itself cites (otzar.org t=34605) lists \"גרוזנברג-התבור-נחלת בנימין\" as a SINGLE colloquial AREA label — one of ~8 Gur shtiblach (alongside פרץ, ברזילי, הקישון, פלורנטין, אחד העם, נוה שאנן, קהל חסידים) — with no house number. The \"14\" is unsourced; it appears reverse-engineered from the present-day business at גרוזנברג 14 (accountant סרוסי יעקב, confirmed via d.co.il), which is circular. The research's own correctedAddress and gaps fields honestly admit the number is unverified, but the top-level \"address\":\"גרוזנברג 14\" + mapErrorFlag:\"wrong-number\" still asserts a precise pin the evidence cannot bear. Correct flag should be \"no-verified-number\" / area-only, not \"wrong-number\".",
        "sourceQuality": "Mixed but mostly genuine. All 5 cited URLs resolve and support their NARROW claims: (1) he.wikipedia חסידות גור — confirms founder יצחק מאיר אלתר and \"7 בתי כנסת בתש\"ט/1949\", but does NOT mention Grozenberg and does NOT tie אברמוביץ specifically to TA. (2) otzar forum t=34605 — confirms the area label \"גרוזנברג-התבור-נחלת בנימין\" but gives NO house number. (3) rabanut.co.il — describes a CURRENTLY ACTIVE Gur synagogue at פיירברג 17 (gabbai שלמה שוירץ / \"שוורץ\"), a DIFFERENT address; using it as a source for a CLOSED Grozenberg shtibel is loose. (4) d.co.il — confirms גרוזנברג 14 is today the accountant סרוסי יעקב (current commercial use, not historic shtibel evidence). (5) כרם ישראל wiki — area-level land-purchase context only. Sources are forum/Wikipedia-derived (low-tier for a precise address), adequate for dynasty + area, inadequate for a building.",
        "dynastyCheck": "SOLID. Gur (גור) attribution is fully supported by both Glatter (dedicated Gur beit-midrash + ישיבת חידושי הרי\"ם entries) and the open web. Gur was indeed the largest Hasidic presence in interwar Tel Aviv. No conflation of dynasty.",
        "founderCheck": "Founder is REAL but mis-leveled (standard dynasty-founder-vs-institution conflation). רבי יצחק מאיר אלתר (\"חידושי הרי\"ם\", d. 1866 in Góra Kalwaria, Poland) genuinely founded the Gur dynasty — but he died ~80+ years before any Tel Aviv shtibel existed and has ZERO connection to this address. The claim correctly separates him as dynasty-founder and names יהודה מאיר אברמוביץ (real; TA deputy mayor 1955–71, MK for Gur) as the local TA representative — Glatter agrees verbatim. So the founder line is defensible as written (dynasty founder labeled as such), but must not be read as the institution's founder.",
