        "addressCheck": "PARTIALLY SUPPORTED, but the address is the weakest link. Glatter does NOT give any street or house number for the Zlatopol-Chortkov / Tzvi Aryeh Twersky entry (it is classified only as \"rebbe-home,\" origin Vienna, year 1938, with no street/number/archival file). So Glatter provides NO support for \"Nachmani 41.\" The number 41 and the synagogue-at-this-building claim rest ENTIRELY on a single secondary commercial source, the Golden Lotus real-estate building-history page (golden-lotus.co.il/nachmani41), which does state: \"Twersky established a modest synagogue on Nachmani Street 41, which served him and his followers until his death.\" That page resolves and does support the claim verbatim. HOWEVER, the two scholarly/encyclopedic sources (Hebrew Wikipedia and Hamichlol) independently and consistently say he lived on \"מרכז בעלי מלאכה\" (Merkaz Ba'alei Melacha) street — NOT Nachmani — and neither mentions Nachmani 41 at all. So there is a genuine source conflict: the only thing tying this rebbe to Nachmani 41 is one non-academic real-estate page; the address is single-sourced and uncorroborated, and the rebbe's documented residence per encyclopedias is a different street.",
        "sourceQuality": "Mixed. Hebrew Wikipedia and Hamichlol (which mirrors Wikipedia) are solid for the biography and both resolve. Both confirm: dynasty Zlatipoli→Chortkov, father Mordechai Yosef of Zlatipoli, descent from Yochanan Twersky/Rachmastrivka, marriage to Chava bat Yisrael Friedman of Chortkov, Dachau 1938, became Chortkov rebbe 1957 after his nephew R. Shlomo's death, died 18 Av 1968, buried Mount of Olives, grandson Chaim Michael Biberfeld in Tottenham, London. The Kedem auction source (cited for the aliyah certificate) returned HTTP 403 and could NOT be independently verified by me; its title indicates Vienna, תרצ\"ט/1939. The load-bearing Nachmani-41-synagogue claim comes ONLY from golden-lotus.co.il, a commercial building-history/real-estate site — not an academic or archival source — and is uncorroborated by any encyclopedic source.",
        "corrections": "1) ADDRESS: Drop or heavily qualify \"Nachmani 41\" as the synagogue site. Glatter gives no street/number for this entry, so the academic index does NOT support 41 (or any number). Hebrew Wikipedia and Hamichlol say the rebbe lived on Merkaz Ba'alei Melacha St., not Nachmani. The Nachmani 41 synagogue is attested by ONLY one secondary commercial source (Golden Lotus). State it as \"single-source, unverified\" rather than \"high confidence.\" 2) CONFIDENCE: \"high\" is too strong given the address rests on one non-academic source that conflicts with the encyclopedic residence. Lower to \"low/medium.\" 3) GLATTER MATCH: The research correctly notes Glatter has no street/number, but then frames Nachmani 41 as merely supplied by an \"independent source\" — that source is a real-estate listing page, not an archival/academic one; this should not be presented as authoritative confirmation. 4) FOUNDING YEAR: The Glatter \"1938\" is the rebbe's arrival/origin year (Vienna→Israel after Dachau), NOT a synagogue founding date — the research correctly does not treat 1938 as the founding year; founding \"late 1950s\" is plausible (he became rebbe 1957) but is itself only inferable from the Golden Lotus phrasing, not documented. 5) DEATH DATE: Golden Lotus says \"September 1968\"; Wikipedia/Hamichlol give 18 Av 5728 = 12 August 1968. Use the encyclopedic date (August). 6) DYNASTY LINEAGE: Minor — Tzvi Aryeh is more precisely a descendant of Yochanan of Rachmastrivka through David Twersky of Zlatipoli (Yochanan was effectively great-grandfather, with David then Mordechai Yosef in between); Wikipedia loosely calls Yochanan his grandfather, so the JSON's phrasing matches the cited source but is genealogically compressed. 7) The marriage is to Chava (some sources spell Chaya) bat Yisrael Friedman of Chortkov — consistent. No image was claimed, so no fabricated portrait — correctly flagged as a gap.",
        "dynastyCheck": "HOLDS. Zlatopol (Zlatipoli) is a genuine branch of the Chernobyl→Rachmastrivka (Twersky) lineage, and Tzvi Aryeh's marriage into the Friedman family tied him to Chortkov (Ruzhin line). The dual Zlatopol-Chortkov leadership (became Chortkov rebbe in 1957 after R. Shlomo Friedman's death) is confirmed by Wikipedia, Hamichlol, and Glatter. No conflation of distinct people detected; the founder is one real, well-documented person, not a composite.",
        "founderCheck": "REAL and correctly identified. Rabbi Tzvi Aryeh Twersky (1890 – 12 Aug 1968), son of Mordechai Yosef the Zlatipoli rebbe, grandson/descendant of Yochanan Twersky of Rachmastrivka, married Chava bat R. Yisrael Friedman of Chortkov, imprisoned in Dachau 1938 and freed by Chortkov hasidim, immigrated via Poland, settled in Tel Aviv, conducted tisch in Ruzhin style, authored \"HaTov ve-HaTachlit\" (1933) and \"Emunah ve-Da'at,\" became Chortkov rebbe 1957, buried on the Mount of Olives, grandson Chaim Michael Biberfeld serves as Zlatopol-Chortkov rebbe in Tottenham, London. The PERSON is solidly documented; the only weakly-supported link is tying him specifically to the Nachmani 41 building.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "DEFENSIBLE but imprecise. Glatter's \"1938\" is the rebbe's arrival/origin marker (post-Dachau immigration; Kedem certificate suggests 1939/תרצ\"ט for Vienna), not a building founding. The JSON's \"late 1950s\" for the synagogue is a reasonable inference (rebbe-ship from 1957, active until death 1968) but is documented only by the Golden Lotus page's loose phrasing; no source gives an exact founding year. The note that the precise founding year is undocumented is itself accurate and should be retained.",
