      "verdict": {
        "overallVerdict": "mostly-solid",
        "addressCheck": "The synagogue is REAL and the Chabad attribution is sound, but the house number is contested and Glatter gives ZERO support for it. Sources split: the COL article and the official amuta registration (GuideStar corporation #580080406, named \"אגודה בית כנסת חב\"ד עין יעקב 11 תל אביב\") say \"עין יעקב 11\"; Hebrew Wikipedia says \"עין יעקב 13\". The research picked 11 and flagged the 13 discrepancy honestly. Glatter's ONLY Chabad entry is a generic \"shtibel\" (rebbe Yosef Yitzchak Schneersohn, origin Crown Heights) with NO street, NO number, NO archival reference, plus the explicit quote that Chabad \"did not strike deep roots in Tel Aviv ... moved its center to Kfar Chabad.\" So Glatter neither confirms nor denies the Ein Yaakov address and cannot override the open-web reasoning (there is no archival file number to override with). The address rests entirely on open web, exactly as the research states. Bottom line: 11 is defensible (official registry outranks Wikipedia) but genuinely ambiguous (11 vs 13) and unverifiable through Glatter.",
        "sourceQuality": "Mixed but mostly legitimate. (1) Hebrew Wikipedia synagogue article resolves and supports founding 1903, Chabad, Slonim, Yudagin, and Gershon Inbund murals, BUT gives the address as \"עין יעקב 13\" and Slonim's death as תרצ\"ו/1936, both contradicting the claim. (2) COL Chabad-news article resolves and supports \"עין יעקב 11\", founding 1903-1904, Slonim, \"major Chabad center\"; it does NOT mention a Wexler namesake or Nachum Gutman. (3) GuideStar registry #580080406 is real and confirms the amuta name embeds \"עין יעקב 11\". (4) Wikipedia Slonim biography is real but CONTRADICTS the claim's dates. (5) Wikimedia Commons image file is real, exists, CC0, a genuine ~1910 portrait photograph (not a gravestone). Glatter is authoritative for archival house numbers but is entirely SILENT on this site, so it adds no corroboration. The research's own \"gaps\" section is unusually honest about the 11-vs-13 split and the un-archived mural attribution.",
        "founderCheck": "Founder Rabbi Shneur Zalman Slonim is a REAL, documented figure specifically tied to THIS synagogue/address — Wikipedia, hamichlol, and COL all credit him with opening the Ohel Yitzchak synagogue for Chabad chassidim in Neve Shalom, Jaffa. Genuine site-specific tie, not a dynasty-elsewhere conflation. Glatter does NOT name him (its Chabad entry names only distant rebbe Yosef Yitzchak Schneersohn), so the founder is web-supported only, consistent with the claim. CONFLATION ERROR: the claim says he was appointed by \"רבי שניאור זלמן מלובלין (בעל תורת חסד)\". The \"Torat Chesed\" author was Rabbi Shneur Zalman FRADKIN (Rabbi of Lublin/Brisk); the claim mangles his surname into a place-epithet and drops \"Fradkin.\"",
        "foundingYearCheck": "Founding year 1903-1904 (תרס\"ג-תרס\"ד) is DEFENSIBLE. COL gives 1903-1904; Wikipedia gives 1903. Both predate Tel Aviv's 1909 founding, so the claim's framing \"about five years before Tel Aviv, under Ottoman rule\" holds. No anachronism.",
        "dynastyCheck": "Chabad attribution HOLDS, well supported by all web sources and consistent with Glatter (which has a Chabad entry). The dynastyBackground narrative (Alter Rebbe founding, Schneersohn dynasty, Hebron Chabad families, Menucha Rachel/Slonim lineage) is broadly accurate. Slonim's lineage is sound: born in Hebron, son of R. Mordechai Dov Slonim, \"from the children of Rebbetzin Menucha Rachel\" (the Alter Rebbe's granddaughter).",
        "imageCheck": "PASS. The cited image (Wikimedia Commons File:סלונים, שניאור זלמן.jpg) is a REAL file that resolves, a genuine ~1910 portrait photograph of Rabbi Shneur Zalman Slonim, licensed CC0 1.0 (public domain). NOT a gravestone, NOT a fabricated URL. Caption accurate.",
        "flaggedClaims": [
          "DEATH YEAR WRONG: Claim says Slonim died 1926 (תרפ\"ו) and served ~36 years. All sources (Wikipedia, hamichlol, chabad.info) give death as תרצ\"ו/1936 with 37 years of service. Off by ten years; Hebrew date wrong (תרפ\"ו vs correct תרצ\"ו). The research anchored to COL's internally inconsistent '1900-1926' tenure.",
          "BIRTH YEAR WRONG: Claim gives birth 1842. No source supports it — Wikipedia gives 1862 (תרכ\"ב); hamichlol gives 1822 (likely typo for 1862). 1842 appears interpolated. The '1842-1926' lifespan is doubly wrong.",
          "APPOINTER NAME CONFLATED: 'רבי שניאור זלמן מלובלין (בעל תורת חסד)' should be Rabbi Shneur Zalman FRADKIN, the 'Torat Chesed' author (Rabbi of Lublin/Brisk). Surname dropped, replaced with a place-epithet.",
          "NAMESAKE UNVERIFIED: The story that the synagogue is named after donor Levi Yitzchak Wexler who gave 70 napoleon is NOT in the cited COL or Wikipedia sources; uncorroborated, possibly fabricated.",
          "MURAL ATTRIBUTION PARTLY UNSUPPORTED: Wikipedia supports Gershon Inbund as muralist, but the added claim that Nachum Gutman 'painted the building in the 1930s' is not in cited sources and is flagged un-archived even by the research.",
          "ADDRESS NUMBER UNRESOLVED: 'עין יעקב 11' (COL/GuideStar) vs 'עין יעקב 13' (Wikipedia); Glatter provides no house number for any Chabad site, so it cannot adjudicate."
        ],
        "corrections": "1) Slonim's death year is 1936 (י\"א בתמוז תרצ\"ו), NOT 1926 — fix the lifespan and change 'thirty-six years' to ~37 years of service. 2) Birth year is 1862 (תרכ\"ב), NOT 1842 — drop the unsupported 1842. 3) The appointing rabbi is Rabbi Shneur Zalman FRADKIN ('Torat Chesed', Rabbi of Lublin), not a person named 'Shneur Zalman of Lublin.' 4) Mark the Wexler-70-napoleon namesake and the Nachum Gutman mural claim as UNVERIFIED (not in cited sources) — source them or downgrade to 'reportedly.' 5) State explicitly that Glatter does NOT support the Ein Yaakov address/house number (its sole Chabad entry is a street-less shtibel noting Chabad relocated to Kfar Chabad); the address rests on open web only. 6) The 11-vs-13 house-number conflict is unresolved by any archival source — keep 11 (official amuta registry) but note it is unconfirmable via Glatter. 7) Lower confidence from 'high' to 'medium' given the multiple date errors."
