        "dynastyCheck": "HOLDS. Ozarov-Chentshin (אוז'רוב-חנצ'ין) is a genuine combined court. Rabbi Moshe Yechiel Halevi Epstein led both because he was the maternal grandson of Rabbi Chaim Shmuel of Chentshin (Chęciny) — confirmed verbatim by Hebrew Wikipedia (\"שימש גם כאדמו\"רה של שושלת חנצ'ין... נכדו מצד אמו של האדמו\"ר חיים שמואל מחנצ'ין\"). Glatter independently lists him under 'אוז'רוב (Ozarov)'. The map label 'חנצין' correctly resolves to this same court. Dynasty attribution is the strongest part of the claim.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "DEFENSIBLE as the aliyah year. 17 Tammuz 5713 = 1953 (Hebrew Wikipedia, MyTzadik). Matches Glatter's year '1953' and origin 'New York' (he immigrated from the US). NOTE: English Wikipedia says \"1952\" for relocation to Tel Aviv — minor source discrepancy; 1953 (aliyah) is well attested. Caveat: 1953 marks his aliyah, not the documented founding of any institution at the stated address; he reached Tel Aviv only after the Chazon Ish died (Cheshvan 5714, late 1953).",
        "founderCheck": "REAL person, correctly identified. Rabbi Moshe Yechiel Halevi Epstein (b. 29 Dec 1889 / תר\"ן; d. 1 Shevat 5731 = 27 Jan 1971, Tel Aviv), Ozarov-Chentshin Rebbe, member of Moetzet Gedolei HaTorah, Israel Prize for rabbinic literature 1968, author of Esh Dat and Be'er Moshe. All confirmed across Hebrew/English Wikipedia, OU.org, MyTzadik. But he is tied to the DYNASTY and to Tel Aviv generally — NOT to Yehuda HaLevi 8. He is documented at Rothschild Blvd (residence + synagogue at Rothschild x Luntz).",
