        "addressCheck": "PARTIAL. The STREET (\"אחד העם\" / Achad Ha'am) is well supported for Chortkov by open web (he.wikipedia \"שלמה פרידמן\": \"קבע את מושבו ברחוב אחד העם\"; \"נחום מרדכי פרידמן\"; ynet; czortkow.org.il) — multiple independent sources place both Nachum Mordechai and his son Shlomo Friedman on Achad Ha'am St in central Tel Aviv. The HOUSE NUMBER \"69\" is NOT supported by any source. No web source and NO archival/Glatter reference gives any number for the Chortkov rebbe-home. Glatter does NOT list a street at all for Chortkov (\"רבי נחום מרדכי ובנו רבי שלמה פרידמן ... צ'ורטקוב ... וינה ... 1939\" — no street, no number, no תיק). The claim's own mapErrorFlag \"wrong-number\"/correctedAddress correctly concedes this. So: street = defensible; number 69 = fabricated/unverified.",
        "sourceQuality": "Mixed but mostly legitimate. The cited he.wikipedia, Hamichlol, Chabadpedia, ynet, and Wikimedia Commons URLs all resolve and genuinely support the founder/dynasty/street/year claims — no fabricated source URLs detected. Weakness: every cited source gives ONLY the street, never a house number, so the entire address-precision layer rests on no evidentiary base. The Belz \"63\" figure has no source in the cited material either (he.wikipedia \"אהרן רוקח\" says only \"קבע את ביתו ברחוב אחד העם במרכז תל אביב\" with NO number) — so \"63\" is as unverified as \"69\".",
        "dynastyCheck": "HOLDS. Chortkov (צ'ורטקוב) is correctly described as a central branch of the Ruzhin dynasty, founded by Rabbi David Moshe Friedman (son of Israel of Ruzhin) who settled in Chortkov, Eastern Galicia. Confirmed by Chabadpedia and he.wikipedia. The Friedman/Chortkov attribution to Nachum Mordechai and his son Shlomo is consistent across web and Glatter. No conflation with the neighboring Zlatopil-Chortkov line (Tzvi Aryeh Twersky), which Glatter lists separately and which actually only later assumed the Chortkov mantle after Shlomo Friedman's death — the research correctly keeps these distinct.",
        "founderCheck": "REAL and tied to the location. Rabbi Nachum Mordechai Friedman (1874–1946), Admor of Chortkov, fled Vienna, arrived 1939, settled in Tel Aviv and opened a small synagogue; succeeded by his son Rabbi Shlomo Friedman (1894–1958) who \"established his residence on Achad Ha'am St.\" Both are documented historical figures (he.wikipedia, Hamichlol, jewiki). Glatter independently confirms: \"רבי נחום מרדכי ובנו רבי שלמה פרידמן ... צ'ורטקוב ... וינה ... 1939.\" Founder is specifically tied to Achad Ha'am STREET (not merely the dynasty elsewhere), though not to any specific number.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "DEFENSIBLE. 1939 is the arrival/establishment year and is confirmed by BOTH Glatter (\"... 1939\") and he.wikipedia (arrived 5699/1939). No anachronism. (Note: the dynasty's own 1865 founding in Chortkov is also correct — תרכ\"ה = 5625 = 1865; the Chabadpedia auto-summary mis-rendered this as 1885, but the Hebrew date is 1865, matching the claim.)",
        "imageCheck": "VALID. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nuchem_Mordechai_Chortkov.jpg exists and is a genuine PORTRAIT photograph of Rabbi Nuchem Mordechai Friedman of Chortkov (1874–1946) — NOT a gravestone, not a fabricated URL. License is Public Domain (PD-Russia art. 1281 + URAA, PD in US). Caption is accurate. The upload URL pattern matches the Commons file. This is a real, free-licensed portrait correctly matched to the subject.",
        "flaggedClaims": [
          "House number '69' is FABRICATED — no web source and no Glatter/archival reference supports any house number for the Chortkov rebbe-home; only the bare street 'אחד העם' is documented.",
          "Belz house number '63' is ALSO unverified — he.wikipedia 'אהרן רוקח' gives only 'רחוב אחד העם' with no number; Glatter likewise gives Belz the street with no number. The claim presents '63' as if documented ('מתועד סביב מספר 63') but cites nothing concrete.",
          "GLATTER vs RESEARCH on Achad Ha'am: Glatter assigns the street 'אחד העם' explicitly to BELZ (Aharon Rokeach, 1944) and assigns NO street to Chortkov. The research's assertion that Glatter 'records' Chortkov on Achad Ha'am is false — Glatter does not. However this is not a true contradiction: independent open-web sources DO place Chortkov on Achad Ha'am, so both rebbes plausibly lived on the same street. The research's framing that 'both at adjacent numbers' is speculation, since neither number is sourced.",
          "Background detail 'מרכז החסידות... בית המדרש ההיסטורי במאה שערים... שהוקם ב-1906' was NOT confirmed by Chabadpedia (which lists current centers in London/Antwerp/Manchester and no Meah Shearim 1906 datum). Treat as unverified background, not load-bearing.",
