        "gaps": "המספר \"31\" ברחוב כפר גלעדי אינו מאומת באף מקור ארכיוני או אקדמי שאיתרתי — גלאטר אינו מתעד כתובת בכפר גלעדי כלל, ומתעד את מודז'יץ רק בדיזנגוף 36. תחנת כפר גלעדי מבוססת על משפט יחיד בוויקיפדיה (\"ליליינבלום, אחר כך כפר גלעדי, אחר כך דיזנגוף\") ללא מספר בית, שנים מדויקות או הפניה ארכיונית; לכן רמת הביטחון בינונית. תווית המפה \"טשורטקוב/מודזיץ\" שגויה בחלקה: צ'ורטקוב היא שושלת נפרדת שמרכזה היה באחד העם פינת חשמונאים, ואין לה קשר לכפר גלעדי — סביר שמדובר בערבוב שתי חצרות. הקשר הנכון לכפר גלעדי הוא מודז'יץ בלבד. לא נמצא מקור עצמאי המאשר את עצם קיומו של בית כנסת פעיל בכתובת זו כיום (הסטטוס \"moved\" משקף את מעבר המרכז לדיזנגוף)."
      },
      "verdict": {
        "overallVerdict": "mostly-solid",
        "addressCheck": "SPLIT VERDICT. The STREET is supported; the HOUSE NUMBER is not. The Kfar Giladi station is real: the correct Wikipedia article — \"יהדות דתית בתל אביב-יפו\" (Religious Judaism in TLV-Jaffa) — states verbatim \"הוא הקים תחילה בית מדרש ברחוב לילינבלום, לאחר מכן ברחוב כפר גלעדי ולאחריו ברחוב דיזנגוף\" (first Lilienblum, then Kfar Giladi, then Dizengoff). So the corrected address \"רחוב כפר גלעדי\" is defensible. BUT the house number \"31\" is fabricated/unsupported: no Wikipedia, Yeshiva.org.il, or Glatter source gives any number on Kfar Giladi; the JSON itself honestly flags this. Glatter does NOT list Kfar Giladi at all — Glatter only documents Modzhitz at Dizengoff 36 (file 1870360). Glatter and the open web do NOT conflict here: Glatter simply records the later permanent center (Dizengoff 36), while Wikipedia records the earlier transitional station (Kfar Giladi). Both are correct for their respective phases. Net: street = real, number \"31\" = drop it.",
        "sourceQuality": "Mixed. The PRIMARY cited URL is BROKEN/WRONG: source #1 points to \"יהדות אורתודוקסית בתל אביב-יפו\" which returns HTTP 404 — the actual article is titled \"יהדות דתית בתל אביב-יפו\" (the article was renamed). The load-bearing Lilienblum/Kfar Giladi/Dizengoff sentence does exist, but at the corrected URL. Source #2 (שמואל אליהו טאוב Wikipedia) resolves and confirms biography BUT notably lists only Lilienblum→Dizengoff, omitting Kfar Giladi entirely — so the Kfar Giladi claim rests on a SINGLE sentence in one article, exactly as the JSON's \"gaps\" admits. Source #3 (Yeshiva.org.il) returned 403 to direct fetch but is corroborated via search: confirms Dizengoff 36 founded 1957. Source #4 (Glatter PDF) is a real academic source. Source #5 (Commons image) is real. Glatter index internally consistent with the JSON's glatterMatch.",
        "corrections": "1) FIX THE SOURCE URL: replace dead \"יהדות_אורתודוקסית_בתל_אביב-יפו\" (404) with the live \"יהדות_דתית_בתל_אביב-יפו\". 2) DROP the house number \"31\" — fabricated, supported by no source; render address as \"רחוב כפר גלעדי, תל אביב\" with no number. 3) The mapErrorFlag \"wrong-dynasty\" is CORRECT and well-justified: the map label conflating \"צ'ורטקוב/מודזיץ\" mixes two unrelated dynasties — Chortkov (R. Nachum Mordechai Friedman, aliyah 1939 from Vienna) was at אחד העם פינת חשמונאים per both Wikipedia and Glatter, never on Kfar Giladi. Kfar Giladi belongs to Modzhitz only. 4) Minor: foundingYear framing is fine (\"mid-1930s\") but note the Kfar Giladi station has NO documented years — present it as undated. 5) Origin nuance: Glatter lists Modzhitz origin as \"קראטשוב (Kratshov)\" (father's emigration point) while Wikipedia gives birthplace Lublin 1905; not a contradiction but worth a footnote.",
        "dynastyCheck": "CORRECT. Modzhitz is a genuine Polish Hasidic dynasty (branch of Kozhnitz-Zvolin), founded by R. Israel Taub (\"Divrei Yisrael\") of Modrzyc, famed for its niggun tradition. R. Shmuel Eliyahu Taub (1905-1984) was indeed the 3rd Admor, grandson of R. Israel, son of R. Shaul Yedidya Elazar. All accurate. The dynasty attribution to the Kfar Giladi beit midrash holds and is the ONLY correct dynasty for that street (Chortkov is a separate, wrongly-conflated dynasty).",
        "founderCheck": "REAL and correctly tied to the institution. R. Shmuel Eliyahu Taub personally established the Kfar Giladi beit midrash as a transitional station — Wikipedia ties HIM specifically to this street, not merely the dynasty elsewhere. He is not a generic dynasty stand-in. Confirmed: immigrated 1935 with his father R. Shaul Yedidya Elazar Taub, became Admor 1947 after father's death, recognized \"יקיר תל אביב\" in 1978. Born 1905 (Lublin per Wikipedia, not stated in claim), died 1984 Tel Aviv. All founder facts check out.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "DEFENSIBLE but soft. The \"mid-1930s\" framing is reasonable (aliyah 1935), but NO source gives a specific year for the Kfar Giladi station itself — it is undated, sitting between the Lilienblum start and the Dizengoff 36 center (founded 1957 per Yeshiva.org.il). The claim honestly presents this as an undated intermediate phase. No anachronism: he was active and resident in TLV from 1935, so a mid/late-1930s-1950s Kfar Giladi station is chronologically coherent.",
        "imageCheck": "VALID. File:Shmuel_Eliyahu_Taub_MODZITZER.jpg exists on Wikimedia Commons, is a genuine formal PORTRAIT photograph of the rabbi (not a gravestone, not a fabricated URL), licensed Public Domain (PD-Poland), sourced from modzitz.org, uploaded 2007. Caption accurate (1905-1984). Passes all checks.",
