        "gaps": "מספר הבית המדויק אינו מאומת: המפה אומרת \"11\", ומקור אינטרנט אחד הזכיר \"ליליינבלום 16\" בהקשר ויז'ניץ אך לא ניתן לאמתו; רשומת גלאטר על ליליינבלום אינה כוללת מספר בית או אסמכתא ארכיונית. לכן ייתכן שגם מספר הבית שגוי (mapErrorFlag המרכזי הוא wrong-dynasty; ייתכן בנוסף wrong-number). הביטחון 'בינוני' משום שהשיוך לוויז'ניץ מגובה היטב (גלאטר + הארץ), אך זיהוי המספר 11 כתובת מדויקת אינו מבוסס. ה-PDF של גלאטר סירב להיטען (ECONNREFUSED) ולכן הסתמכתי על הציטוטים שסופקו ועל אימות צולב מהעיתונות וויקיפדיה. ייתכן בלבול בין שני אחי הגר: רבי אליעזר הגר ('דמשק אליעזר', פיארברג 35) לעומת רבי חיים מאיר הגר (ליליינבלום); הסיפור כאן מתייחס לחיים מאיר הגר על פי 'הארץ'."
      },
      "verdict": {
        "overallVerdict": "mostly-solid",
        "addressCheck": "PARTIAL / CONFLICTED. The STREET (Liliyenblum) is well-supported as a Vizhnitz site, but the house number 11 is NOT supported by any source and is almost certainly wrong; the CLAIM itself concedes this and flags wrong-number. Two independent open-web sources (Hebrew Wikipedia article 'Religious Jewry in Tel Aviv-Yafo' and a search snippet of the same) give Liliyenblum 16 for Chaim Meir Hager, so the corrected house number should be 16 (medium confidence), not 'unknown'. CRITICAL WHO-CONFLICT: the Glatter index ties the Liliyenblum rebbe-home to Eliezer Hager ('Damesek Eliezer', arrived 1944, d.1946), grouping it with Fierberg 35; the CLAIM instead names his brother Chaim Meir Hager ('Amrei Chaim', 4th rebbe) per Haaretz 2009 + Wikipedia. This is a genuine source disagreement, not fabrication. Most likely BOTH brothers used Liliyenblum at different times (Eliezer 1944-46 per Glatter; Chaim Meir 1947-51 per press). Glatter gives NO house number for Liliyenblum, so Glatter does not override the number question; on the number, open web (16) beats the map's 11.",
        "founderCheck": "REAL person, but ATTRIBUTION CONTESTED. Chaim Meir Hager (Amrei Chaim, b.1887 Vyzhnytsia, d.1972 Bnei Brak) is a real, central 20th-century rebbe, the 4th of Vizhnitz, later a Moetzet Gedolei HaTorah member - all correct. He IS tied to Liliyenblum by Haaretz (2009) and Hebrew Wikipedia ('Religious Jewry in Tel Aviv-Yafo'), which name him at Liliyenblum 16 with a Friday-night tisch drawing hundreds. HOWEVER the Glatter index names the OTHER brother, Eliezer Hager, at Liliyenblum. The CLAIM's narration that 'Glatter's Liliyenblum record describes a Vizhnitz rebbe-home' subtly implies Glatter supports Chaim Meir; it does not - Glatter's index entry says Eliezer. Conflation risk is high because the dynasty reuses the name 'Chaim Meir Hager' (a grandson b.1972 is a different person, which I confirmed). Founder is real and the Haaretz/Wikipedia tie to Chaim Meir is defensible, but the claim overstates Glatter's backing.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "SHAKY framing. Calling tasch/1947 a 'founding year' is loose - this was an interim residence, not a founded institution, and the CLAIM acknowledges this. Immigration year: sources conflict. Heb. Wikipedia (Chaim Meir) and Haaretz-adjacent sources say Elul 5707 = Sept 1947; but Hamichlol/another Wikipedia line says he came 'a year after' Eliezer (1944), i.e. ~1945/tav-shin-vav. The CLAIM's '1947' is the more commonly cited date and is defensible. The 1943 Vizhnitz-Otynia shtibel petition (Glatter, file chativa 8) is real but belongs to Eliezer's circle, not Chaim Meir specifically - the CLAIM correctly separates it. Move to Bnei Brak: CLAIM says 'early 1950s'; Wikipedia says he BEGAN Shikun Vizhnitz in tav-shin-chet (1948) and open-web says the move/relocation completed ~1951. So 'early 1950s' for the completed move is acceptable, but the start was 1948.",
        "dynastyCheck": "CORRECT. Vizhnitz (origin Vyzhnytsia, Bukovina; branch of the Ruzhin-Kosov house; founded by R. Menachem Mendel Hager, the 'Tzemach Tzadik') is accurately described. The map's 'Lublin/Modzhitz' label for this Liliyenblum site is wrong - Glatter lists Lublin only as a generic shtibel and Modzhitz firmly at Dizengoff 36 (file 1870360), neither on Liliyenblum. So the mapErrorFlag 'wrong-dynasty' is justified: the correct dynasty is Vizhnitz. The dynastyBackground paragraph is historically sound.",
        "imageCheck": "VALID. File:Imreichaim.JPG on Wikimedia Commons is a REAL, present file - a portrait photograph of Rabbi Chaim Meir Hager (Amrei Chaim) of Vizhnitz, NOT a gravestone, not a fabricated URL. Dual-licensed CC BY-SA 3.0 / GFDL, 'Unknown author', uploaded to he.wikipedia 2007, moved to Commons 2012, used across multiple Wikipedias. License and caption in the CLAIM are accurate. The portrait matches the founder the CLAIM names (Chaim Meir), which is internally consistent with the CLAIM but note it does NOT match Glatter's Eliezer attribution.",
        "sourceQuality": "Mixed but mostly legitimate. Haaretz 2009 article exists and per search snippet genuinely contains the quoted Liliyenblum/Chaim Meir/Friday-tisch passage, but full text is paywalled - quote confirmable only via snippet, not direct read. Glatter PDF (BGU Iyunim 36) is real and authoritative but refused to load (ECONNREFUSED) on every fetch attempt; I relied on the supplied index plus search snippets. The supplied Glatter index assigns Liliyenblum to ELIEZER Hager, contradicting the CLAIM's implication that Glatter backs Chaim Meir. The two Wikipedia bio articles are real and consistent (Eliezer: arrived 1944, d.1946; Chaim Meir: 4th rebbe, immigrated 1947, no Liliyenblum mention in his own bio). The 'Religious Jewry in Tel Aviv-Yafo' Wikipedia article supplies the key external corroboration for Chaim Meir at Liliyenblum 16.",
        "corrections": "1) HOUSE NUMBER: change '11' to '16'. Open-web sources (Heb. Wikipedia 'Religious Jewry in Tel Aviv-Yafo') give Chaim Meir Hager at Liliyenblum 16; '11' is unsupported by any source. Keep medium confidence. 2) GLATTER FRAMING: do NOT imply Glatter supports Chaim Meir at Liliyenblum. Glatter's index entry names ELIEZER Hager at Liliyenblum. State explicitly that Glatter (Eliezer) and Haaretz/Wikipedia (Chaim Meir) DISAGREE on which brother; the most likely reconciliation is that both occupied/used Liliyenblum at different times (Eliezer ~1944-46; Chaim Meir ~1947-51). 3) On WHICH IS CORRECT: for the *named founder of the Friday-night tisch that drew hundreds*, the press (Haaretz) and Wikipedia favor Chaim Meir - that specific claim stands. For Glatter's *archival* Liliyenblum record specifically, the correct person is Eliezer, so the CLAIM's sentence 'gam reshumat Glatter al Liliyenblum metaeret beit-admor shel Vizhnitz' should attribute that record to Eliezer, not blur it under Chaim Meir. 4) BNEI BRAK MOVE: note Shikun Vizhnitz was BEGUN in 1948 (tav-shin-chet), with the court center fully relocated by ~1951; 'early 1950s' is acceptable only for the completed transfer. 5) IMMIGRATION: flag the 1945-vs-1947 discrepancy across sources; '1947' is defensible but not unanimous. 6) NAME CONFLATION WARNING: 'Chaim Meir Hager' is reused in the dynasty (a grandson b.1972); ensure the portrait and bio are the elder Amrei Chaim (they are). 7) The map's 'Lublin/Modzhitz' label IS wrong (wrong-dynasty correct); Modzhitz is at Dizengoff 36 per Glatter, not Liliyenblum.",
