        "overallVerdict": "mostly-solid",
        "addressCheck": "YES — Glatter supports the corrected address. The Glatter index entry {dynasty: \"ללוב / לעלוב (Lelov / Lalov)\", type: \"shtibel\", street: \"מוהליבר\", number: \"37\", archivalRef: \"את\\\"ב, תיק 1320370, מסמך 7617848\"} matches the research's correctedAddress exactly. This is independently corroborated on the open web: golden-lotus.co.il (the building file for Mohliver 37 / \"בית יעקב בכור\") states \"בשנת 1959 המבנה חדל לשמש למגורים והחל לשמש כבית המדרש 'בית דוד' של חסידות לעלוב\" — i.e., from 1959 Mohliver 37 served as the \"Beit David\" study hall of Lelov Hasidism. The Glatter source itself is REAL: it is the academic article \"אדמו\\\"רים ברחובות תל אביב 1940–1965\" by Michal Glatter, published in BGU's Iyunim journal (in.bgu.ac.il/bgi/iyunim/36/36-Michal-Glatter.pdf). House number 37 is therefore defensible. The map address זבולון 21 is correctly flagged as NOT Lelov: rabanut.co.il confirms זבולון 21 hosts the Mizrahi/Sephardi synagogue \"כוג'הינוף – היכל מהריך\" (gabbai הרב חיים טבריה), and d.co.il confirms the commercial business \"אריזול\" at the same address. No source ties Zvulun 21 to Lelov. mapErrorFlag \"wrong-number\" and the correction are sound.",
        "sourceQuality": "High. All six cited URLs resolve and support their claims. Wikipedia (חסידות לעלוב, משה מרדכי בידרמן) confirms the dynasty history and that the 6th Rebbe lived in Tel Aviv. rabanut.co.il confirms Zvulun 21 = Sephardi \"כוג'הינוף – היכל מהריך\". project-tlv.info and d.co.il are minor corroborating sources (the only synagogue project-tlv lists on Zvulun is \"אליהו הנביא\" at no. 16, not Lelov — consistent with the no-Lelov-link claim). The load-bearing Glatter academic source is genuine and additionally corroborated by golden-lotus.co.il for Mohliver 37. The Glatter PDF blocked automated fetch (ECONNREFUSED) but its existence, authorship, and Lelov/Mohliver-37 content are confirmed via search snippet and the independent golden-lotus corroboration.",
        "founderCheck": "CONFIRMED real but with a minor chronological tension. רבי דוד בידרמן is the genuine founder of Lelov Hasidism and a documented student of the Seer of Lublin (החוזה מלובלין) — verified on he.wikipedia. However, he was born ~1745/46 and DIED 28 January 1814 (ז' שבט ה'תקע\"ד). The founder is correctly named and correctly tied only to the DYNASTY (not to the Tel Aviv address), which is appropriate.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "Defensible but internally inconsistent. Hebrew Wikipedia lists the dynasty's founding year as ה'תקע\"ה = 5575 = 1815, so \"~1815 for the world court\" matches the standard source. BUT the founder David Biderman died in January 1814 — so a court \"founded in 1815 by David Biderman\" is mildly anachronistic (the 1815 civil-year label derived from תקע\"ה straddles late 1814/1815, and his leadership clearly predates his 1814 death). This is a known quirk of the Wikipedia dating, not a fabrication by the research. The Tel-Aviv shtibel founding date is honestly marked \"unknown\"; web evidence actually pins the Mohliver 37 Beit David use to 1959, which the research did not state but its \"gaps\" note correctly flags as undocumented online.",
        "dynastyCheck": "HOLDS. Lelov (לעלוב/ללוב) is a real, Jerusalem-flavored Polish Hasidic court. The dynastyBackground is accurate: Moshe Biderman (2nd Rebbe) made aliyah to Jerusalem in 1851 (ה'תרי\"א) and died ~2.5 months later; Eliezer Menachem Mendel (3rd Rebbe) was indeed \"the first in the entire Hasidic world to begin his leadership in Jerusalem\"; Moshe Mordechai Biderman (1903–1987) was the 6th Rebbe, lived in Jerusalem then Tel Aviv then Bnei Brak (Shikun Heh). All verified against he.wikipedia. The Glatter index also lists \"לעלוב — רבי משה מרדכי בידרמן — ירושלים — 1942 — rebbe-home\", matching the research's note. The proposed \"ללוב vs עולי לוב (Libya)\" confusion is plausible speculation, appropriately hedged.",
        "imageCheck": "Image correctly OMITTED — good adversarial judgment. The only Wikimedia Commons file for this Rebbe (File:רבי משה מרדכי בידרמן בן רבי שמעון נתן נתע מלעלוב.JPG, CC BY-SA 4.0) is a GRAVESTONE/tombstone photo at the Mount of Olives, NOT a portrait. Using it as a portrait would have been an error. The research's \"gaps\" note (\"לא צורפה תמונת דיוקן... שלא אותרה תמונה בעלת רישיון חופשי מאומת\") is accurate and the right call.",
