      "verdict": {
        "overallVerdict": "mostly-solid",
        "addressCheck": "Glatter supports the dynasty+street pairing: search-engine indexing of the Glatter PDF (in.bgu.ac.il/bgi/iyunim/36/36-Michal-Glatter.pdf) confirms \"Munkacs Hasidim operated on HaAvoda Street.\" HaAvoda street genuinely exists in Tel Aviv's \"Merkaz Ba'alei Melacha\" craftsmen quarter and hosts other Hasidic shtiblach (e.g. Skierniewice/Skarnevitz at HaAvoda 12), so a Munkacs shtibel at HaAvoda 15 is geographically plausible and consistent with neighboring records. CAVEAT: the exact house number \"15\" and the archival reference (תיק 210150 / מסמך 6688054) appear ONLY inside the Glatter source — I could not independently corroborate them anywhere on the open web. They are taken on Glatter's authority, not externally verified. The Glatter index entry and the research JSON agree exactly (shtibel, Munkacs, HaAvoda 15, 1944, origin Budapest, rebbe Rabinowicz), so there is no internal contradiction and mapErrorFlag \"none\" is appropriate.",
        "sourceQuality": "Mixed-to-good. STRONG: (1) Glatter, \"אדמו\\\"רים ברחובות תל אביב 1940-1965\", Iyunim 36, Ben-Gurion Institute — a peer-reviewed academic source; PDF exists and is indexed as placing Munkacs on HaAvoda street (the BGU server intermittently refused direct fetches/ECONNREFUSED, but the article is real and confirmed via Kotar mirror kotar.cet.ac.il and search indexing). (2) Hebrew Wikipedia on Rabinowicz — resolves, confirms biography. (3) Yad Vashem munkacs/rabbi_rabinowitz.asp — resolves and confirms biography (note: cited under host \"wwv.yadvashem.org\", an unusual subdomain, but it resolved). (4) ynet article ryvs11n9p5 — resolves, lists Munkacs among TA dynasties but gives NO address (so it supports the dynasty's TA presence only, not the specific street/number). (5) Wikimedia Commons file — real, free-licensed (CC BY-SA 3.0 / GFDL) portrait of the correct rebbe. No source is fabricated; all five URLs resolve.",
        "founderCheck": "Real and correctly identified (see detail above); correctly NOT claimed to have personally resided at the address.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "1944 is defensible. It matches the Glatter index entry (year 1944) and the archival document; it is also chronologically coherent — Rabinowicz reached Eretz Israel in 1945 per Wikipedia, but the shtibel served local Munkacs CONGREGANTS, so its ~1944 registration does not require the rebbe's presence. No anachronism: this is a community shtibel, and \"1944\" reflects when its activity is documented in the municipal archive, not the rebbe's arrival. Defensible.",
        "dynastyCheck": "Holds. Munkacs (Munkács / Mukachevo, Carpathian Ruthenia) is a genuine Hasidic dynasty; the dynastyBackground is accurate (Minchas Elazar, anti-Zionist stance, succession by son-in-law Rabinowicz, Holocaust destruction, post-war dispersal). Glatter, ynet, and Wikipedia all independently attest a Munkacs presence in mid-century Tel Aviv. No dynasty conflation detected.",
        "imageCheck": "FLAG — image is real but the caption mis-attributes the photographer. The file File:Boruch_Rabinowicz_019.jpg exists on Wikimedia Commons, is a genuine PORTRAIT (not a gravestone) of the correct person (R. Baruch Rabinowicz, the Munkacs Rebbe), and is properly free-licensed (CC BY-SA 3.0 / GFDL). HOWEVER, the research caption credits the photo to \"Roman Vishniac\" — the Commons file page lists the author/photographer as UNKNOWN, with no Vishniac attribution. The \"(צילום: רומן וישניאק)\" credit is fabricated/unsupported and should be removed. The URL itself is valid and resolves.",
        "flaggedClaims": [
