        "overallVerdict": "mostly-solid",
        "addressCheck": "PARTIAL. The house number \"17\" is NOT supported by Glatter. Glatter places the Sadigura center on רחוב פנקס (Pinkas), with NO number and NO mention of Oliphant, and says Sadigura moved its center to Pinkas in the 1960s. \"Oliphant 17\" comes only from open-web sources: the Tel Aviv Religious Council (rabanut.co.il) lists the synagogue at אוליפנט 17 under the combined name \"אביר יעקב – לזכר קדושי אנטופול\", and easy.co.il lists \"אביר יעקב - אוליפנט 17\". So the address is real and well-attested on the open web, but it is a DIFFERENT branch/building than the one Glatter indexes. Independent architectural sources (project-tlv.info, michaelarch.wordpress.com) establish that the Oliphant 17 building is the purpose-built ANTOPOL SURVIVORS' MEMORIAL SYNAGOGUE: cornerstone 1955, built/inaugurated 1960, founded by Antopol native Zalman Zev Shahor, architects Shmuel Mohilever & Moshe Kaufman, famous for its Star-of-David concrete lattice facade. The Sadigura \"Avir Yaakov\" minyan (per Hebrew Wikipedia, led by Rabbi Mordechai Auerbach) relocated INTO this building after the Nachmani synagogue was lost in failed legal battles post-1960. So the two identities genuinely co-exist at Oliphant 17 today — but the research's claim that it moved to \"the SCHOOL BUILDING on Oliphant\" is imprecise/likely wrong: Oliphant 17 is a purpose-built 1960 synagogue, not a former school.",
        "dynastyCheck": "HOLDS. Sadigura attribution for the \"Avir Yaakov\" congregation is directly supported by Hebrew Wikipedia (חסידות סדיגורה), which states verbatim that R. Avraham Yaakov opened a synagogue on Nachmani, that after his death legal battles failed and \"בית המדרש עבר למבנה בית הספר ברחוב אוליפנט. עומד בראשו הרב מרדכי אוירבך, רב קהילת 'אביר יעקב'.\" Sadigura is correctly described as a Ruzhin-house court founded in Sadhora, Bukovina. A kikar.co.il news item confirms Sadigura Hasidim gather in Tel Aviv for the \"Avir Yaakov\" hilula. The dynastyBackground paragraph is accurate.",
        "founderCheck": "REAL but framed loosely. Rabbi Avraham Yaakov Friedman (II), \"the Avir Yaakov,\" is a real historical Sadigura rebbe; his title/book name \"אביר יעקב\" is genuine (the book was compiled after his death and first printed in Jerusalem, תשכ\"ד/1963-64). He is the namesake of the beit-midrash LINE, not the founder of the Oliphant 17 building (that was Zalman Zev Shahor of Antopol). The research correctly distinguishes the namesake/line from the current local leader, Rabbi Mordechai Auerbach (a real figure with his own Hebrew Wikipedia page, appointed by the Ruzhin rebbes, son of R. Shlomo Zalman Auerbach). No fabricated people.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "Original Nachmani synagogue ~1938 is defensible (rebbe arrived כ\"ג אלול תרצ\"ח = 1938; note one source says 1937 — minor source disagreement). The move to Oliphant \"after 1960\" is defensible and honestly hedged; exact year not documented. NOTE Glatter dates the Sadigura rebbe's arrival to 1939 (from Vienna), one to two years later than the Wikipedia-based 1937/1938 — a minor conflict the research does not flag.",
