      "verdict": {
        "overallVerdict": "mostly-solid",
        "addressCheck": "Street YES, number NO. Glatter explicitly places the \"ויז'ניץ־אטינא\" shtibel on רחוב רש\"י (Rashi street) and the gabbai's letter to the Tel Aviv municipality (dated 27 July 1943, summer 1943) requesting the Echad Ha'am school hall for the High Holy Days because the regular Rashi-street space was too cramped is a real, citable archival document (אעת\"א, חטיבה 8, מיכל 1084, תיק 2288). I confirmed this content via multiple web snippets quoting the Glatter article verbatim. However, NO source — neither Glatter nor open web — supports house number \"30\". Glatter gives only the street with no number. The research itself correctly flags this; \"רש\"י 30\" is unsupported and should be dropped. The \"Sokolov\" map label is also unsupported: the only Sokolov in Glatter is \"סוקולוב קאצק\" (R. Mendel Meir Morgenstern, Otwock, 1946, rebbe-home), a different dynasty unconnected to Rashi street. mapErrorFlag \"wrong-dynasty\" is justified.",
        "sourceQuality": "Mixed but adequate. (1) The Glatter PDF (in.bgu.ac.il/bgi/iyunim/36/36-Michal-Glatter.pdf), \"אדמו\"רים ברחובות תל אביב 1940–1965\", Iyunim BiTkumat Yisrael, Ben-Gurion Institute — is a real, peer-reviewed academic source and the correct citation. I could NOT load it directly from this environment (server refused connection / likely region-restricted), but multiple independent web-search snippets quote its exact wording (the 27 July 1943 Vizhnitz-Atina gabbai letter, Echad Ha'am school request, two-shift weekday prayer), so its existence and content are confirmed. (2) Hebrew Wikipedia \"חסידות אוטיניא\" RESOLVES and fully supports the dynasty background: Chaim Hager (d. 1932), son of R. Baruch of Vizhnitz, founded the Otynia court; successors Yisrael Shalom Yosef (murdered May 1944), Moshe (d. 1994), Baruch (current Admor, Bnei Brak). (3) Hebrew Wikipedia \"חסידות ויז'ניץ\" supports Vizhnitz origin. Sources are reliable; the primary source just isn't directly fetchable here.",
        "founderCheck": "The named founder, רבי חיים הגר מאוטיניא, is REAL and correctly identified as founder of the Otynia COURT (Wikipedia confirms: second son of R. Baruch of Vizhnitz, established Otynia, died 1932). But he is the dynasty founder, NOT a figure personally tied to the Tel Aviv Rashi-street shtibel — which the research correctly handles by stating the local shtibel was run by unnamed gabbaim. CONFLATION RISK in Glatter's own index: Glatter's Rashi/ויז'ניץ־אטינא record names \"רבי אליעזר הגר\", and uses that identical name+label for a SEPARATE entry — the Piarberg 35 rebbe-home (= Eliezer Hager \"Damesek Eliezer\", mainline Vizhnitz, 1891–1946, immigrated 19 April 1944, founded the Vizhnitz yeshiva in Tel Aviv). In summer 1943 the Damesek Eliezer was not yet in Israel, so he cannot have led the Rashi shtibel then; the \"רבי אליעזר הגר\" attached to the Rashi entry is most plausibly an index duplication/over-attribution. The research's gap note flags Eliezer Hager but points at the WRONG namesake (Seret-Vizhnitz Eliezer, 1924–2015, Haifa); the more relevant confusion is with the Damesek Eliezer.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "Defensible as stated. The research does NOT assert a true founding year; it says \"active by no later than 1943\" per the gabbai letter, and explicitly marks the exact founding year unknown. This is supported and appropriately hedged — the 27 July 1943 archival letter proves the shtibel was operating by summer 1943. No anachronism.",
        "dynastyCheck": "SUPPORTED. Glatter explicitly labels the Rashi-street shtibel \"ויז'ניץ־אטינא\" (Vizhnitz-Atina/Otynia), and the gabbai letter is from the \"בית הכנסת ויז'ניץ־אטינא\". Otynia is a genuine offshoot of Vizhnitz (Wikipedia confirms). Where Glatter vs. research diverge: Glatter attaches the personal name \"רבי אליעזר הגר\" to this dynasty record, which is questionable (see founderCheck) — but the DYNASTY label itself (Vizhnitz-Otynia) is correct and is Glatter's, so on the dynasty question Glatter governs and the research follows it correctly. The competing \"Sokolov\" map label is wrong.",
        "imageCheck": "N/A — no image/portrait was included in the claim (the research notes no free-licensed portrait of an Otynia Admor was found on Wikimedia Commons). Nothing fabricated; nothing to verify. No gravestone or bogus image URL present.",
