        "addressCheck": "The Glatter index entry is internally consistent and self-corroborating: dynasty \"סטרטין־אשלג (Stratyn-Ashlag)\", rebbe רבי יהודה צבי ברנדווין, origin Jerusalem, year 1944, type shtibel, street הלל הזקן, number 10, archival ref את\"ב תיק 1200100 מסמכים 9115461, 9115389. So Glatter DOES support \"Hillel HaZaken 10\". The research correctly defers to Glatter (number 10) over the community map (number 1) and flags it as wrong-number — this is the right call IF Glatter is authoritative. CAVEAT: I could not independently fetch the BGU PDF (server unreachable: ECONNREFUSED / \"cannot connect to remote server\" on every attempt via WebFetch, curl, and Invoke-WebRequest), so the house number 10, file number 1200100, and the 1944 year rest ENTIRELY on the supplied Glatter index quote. A WebSearch AI snippet of the PDF loosely corroborated that the document mentions Stratyn on Hillel HaZaken and Brandwein as Histadrut religious-dept head, but snippets are not authoritative for an exact house number. NO open-web source ties Brandwein or Stratyn to any Hillel HaZaken address — both Wikipedia articles and the detailed mytzadik/Michlol biographies place his court in Jerusalem's Old City and are silent on Tel Aviv. The address therefore stands or falls on Glatter alone; the research honestly says so in its gaps field.",
        "sourceQuality": "Mixed. The two Hebrew Wikipedia articles (Brandwein-the-kabbalist; Hasidut Stratyn) and the parallel HaMichlol articles are real, resolve, and confirm the core biography. The Wikimedia Commons image File:Zvi.jpg is a REAL public-domain portrait photograph (not a gravestone), a 1962 photo of Brandwein credited to Shloma Pash, released PD by the copyright holder under VRT ticket #2011022710010495 — the image claim is fully valid. The Glatter PDF (Michal Glatter, \"אדמו\\\"רים ברחובות תל אביב 1940-1965\", Iyunim BiTkumat Yisrael 36, BGU) is a genuine peer-reviewed academic source and is the ONLY source for the Tel Aviv address/year, but it was unreachable for direct verification from this environment.",
        "corrections": "1) RELATIONSHIP ERROR (repeated, material): The claim states multiple times that Brandwein was Ashlag's son-in-law (\"חתנו וממשיכו\", \"חתן בעל הסולם\", and \"ישיבת קול יהודה על שם חותנו\"). This is FALSE. Per both Hebrew Wikipedia articles and the detailed mytzadik/Michlol biography, Brandwein was Ashlag's STUDENT and BROTHER-IN-LAW (גיס): Ashlag arranged for Brandwein to marry his wife's sister, Leah bat R. Meir Abramowitz (\"הציע לשדך לו את אחות אשתו, מרת לאה... והרב ברנדווין נשא את מרת לאה לאשה\"). Correct every \"חתן/חתנו\" to \"גיס/תלמיד וגיס\". The yeshiva \"Kol Yehuda\" is named after R. Yehuda Leib Ashlag, who was his teacher/brother-in-law, NOT \"חותנו\" (father-in-law). 2) Burial: sources say Har HaMenuchot / near Ashlag's grave in Jerusalem (Wikimedia metadata says Mount of Olives); claim just says \"נטמן בירושלים\" — acceptable but imprecise. 3) The 1944 founding year and house number 10 are UNCORROBORATED outside Glatter — keep confidence at medium and do not present them as independently confirmed. 4) The dynasty attribution (Stratyn / Stratyn-Ashlag, Galician origin via Brandwein family, founder R. Yehuda Zvi Brandwein the First d. 1844, disciple of R. Uri of Strelisk) is accurate and well-supported. Where Glatter and the community map disagree on the house number, Glatter (10) is the better authority given its archival file citation; the map's \"1\" is plausibly a truncated \"10\".",
        "dynastyCheck": "Holds. Stratyn (Stratyn-Ashlag) is a real Galician Hasidic dynasty; Brandwein was genuinely the Admor of Stratyn in Eretz Yisrael and led his court \"in the way of Ashlag.\" The fusion of Galician Hasidut with Ashlag's Lurianic Kabbalah is accurately described. The founder-of-the-line detail (R. Yehuda Zvi Brandwein I, disciple of R. Uri of Strelisk \"the Saraf\") is correct.",
        "founderCheck": "The person is real and correctly identified: R. Yehuda Zvi Brandwein (1903 Safed – 1969 Jerusalem), Admor of Stratyn, head of the Histadrut religious department from 1957. He is, however, tied to THIS specific Hillel HaZaken 10 address ONLY by Glatter; no open-web source attaches him to that Tel Aviv address (open sources place his court in Jerusalem). His relationship to Ashlag is mis-stated as son-in-law; he was brother-in-law/student.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "1944 is from Glatter only and not independently verifiable here. It is chronologically plausible (Brandwein, b. 1903, was active in Eretz Yisrael by the 1940s; he only moved to the Jerusalem Jewish Quarter in 1967, so an earlier Tel Aviv presence is not contradicted). But it should be presented as Glatter-sourced, medium confidence — not established fact.",
        "imageCheck": "VALID. File:Zvi.jpg on Wikimedia Commons is a genuine black-and-white PORTRAIT photograph (1962) of Yehuda Zvi Brandwein, credited to Shloma Pash, in the public domain (released by copyright holder, VRT ticket #2011022710010495). Not a gravestone, not a fabricated URL. The upload.wikimedia.org URL and caption are accurate.",
