      "verdict": {
        "overallVerdict": "mostly-solid",
        "addressCheck": "PARTIAL. The STREET (Borochov, Tel Aviv) is well supported: the bhol.co.il article (news/741219) verbatim places Rabbi Asher Fishman as one of the first students of kollel Kol Yaakov in the beit midrash of the Rebbe on Borochov Street in Tel Aviv. The HOUSE NUMBER 8 is UNSUPPORTED by every source checked: neither Wikipedia, hamichlol, the bhol article, nor the Glatter index gives any house number for Strikov. The research JSON is honest about this and explicitly flags 8 as an unverified, apparent map-maker estimate. Glatter's Strikov record (Rabbi Avraham Landa, Strikov, Vilna, 1941, type rebbe-home) gives NO street and NO number, so it neither supports nor contradicts the address and cannot be used to confirm Borochov 8. The unrelated Nachalat Avot synagogue at Borochov 24 is correctly identified as a separate Ashkenazi shul, not Strikov.",
        "sourceQuality": "Good and honestly characterized. All 5 cited URLs resolve and support what they are cited for: he.wikipedia (both the Strikov-Hasidut article and the Avraham Avigdor Landa biography) confirms the dynasty chain and biography; bhol.co.il/news/741219 independently confirms the Borochov beit midrash and the Kol Yaakov kollel; hamichlol mirrors Wikipedia; rabanut.co.il confirms Borochov 24 = Nachalat Avot (separate shul). No fabricated or dead URLs; the Glatter quote is reproduced faithfully. Weakness: sources are mostly Wikipedia-family (Wikipedia and hamichlol are not independent of each other), and no archival/primary source pins the address, so the stated medium confidence is appropriately calibrated.",
        "founderCheck": "REAL founder, but with a sourcing error in the parenthetical. Rabbi Aharon Tzvi Landa is the genuine founder of the Strikov dynasty per Wikipedia/hamichlol. However the JSON's gloss son of Rabbi Dov Ber of Bialystok is WRONG: sources say he came from Biala (mi-Biala) and was the son of Rabbi Dov Ber of Biala, NOT Bialystok. Biala and Bialystok are different places; this is a conflation. Separately, the JSON's founder field lists Rabbi Aharon Tzvi Landa as dynasty founder but for THIS Tel Aviv institution the relevant founder is correctly given as the secondary value, Rabbi Avraham Avigdor Nachum Landa, who established the Tel Aviv center. That tie is solid and Glatter-supported.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "1941 is defensible as the immigration year and matches Glatter exactly. The Tel Aviv center properly dates to the 1948 coronation, which the JSON discloses rather than hiding. No anachronism in the year itself.",
        "dynastyCheck": "HOLDS. Strikov (Strykow, Poland) dynasty attribution is correct and Glatter-confirmed (dynasty Strikov, rebbe Rabbi Avraham Landa, origin Vilna, 1941). The Landa-family succession chain (Aharon Tzvi to Elimelech Menachem Mendel to Yaakov Yitzchak Dan to Avraham Avigdor Nachum) is corroborated by Wikipedia/hamichlol. The post-2001 split into Bnei Brak (son-in-law David Brander) and Jerusalem (grandson Yaakov Dan Stern) is confirmed.",
        "imageCheck": "CORRECTLY EXCLUDED. The Wikimedia Commons file (File:האדמור מסטריקוב.JPG) is public domain and is a genuine portrait, but it was photographed 2011-11-22 and is captioned Strikov Rebbe - Bnei Brak, depicting the SUCCESSOR (associated with David Brander), not the Tel Aviv-era Rabbi Avraham Avigdor Landa who died in 2001. The JSON's decision to omit it to avoid misattribution is sound. No fabricated image URL was used.",
        "flaggedClaims": [
