      "verdict": {
        "overallVerdict": "mostly-solid",
        "addressCheck": "PARTIAL. Street \"Tiyomkin\" is well-supported: Hebrew Wikipedia and HaMichlol both state he \"settled on Tiyomkin Street in Tel Aviv\" and opened a small beit midrash near his home. BUT the house number \"9\" is UNVERIFIED by any source. Glatter's entry for this rebbe carries NO street, NO number, and NO archivalRef (unlike the Modzhitz/Munkacs/Vizhnitz/Stratyn/Bohush entries, which all cite Tel Aviv Archive file numbers for their house numbers). So Glatter does NOT support \"9\" — it is silent. No open-web source gives a number either. The \"9\" appears to originate only from a map pin and should be treated as unconfirmed. The JSON's own gaps field honestly admits this.",
        "sourceQuality": "Decent but thin and tertiary. The three cited URLs (Hebrew Wikipedia bio, HaMichlol bio, Hebrew Wikipedia Slonim-Hasidism) all resolve and genuinely support the biographical and dynastic core. They are encyclopedic/tertiary, not archival. No primary or archival source (e.g. Tel Aviv Archive) is cited for the Tel Aviv address — and Glatter, the would-be archival override, provides none here. The book \"Otzar Erchei HaTorah – B'Ahalei Yissachar\" is independently confirmed (Google Books, used-book dealers).",
        "corrections": "1) ADDRESS NUMBER: Change address to \"טיומקין, תל אביב\" (street only) or explicitly mark \"9\" as unverified/map-derived. Neither Glatter nor the web supports \"9\"; Glatter is silent (no street/number/archivalRef for this entry), so it cannot override or confirm. 2) \"REFUSED TO SERVE AS ADMOR\": The story's claim that he \"consistently refused to serve as admor\" (סירב בעקביות לכהן כאדמו\"ר) is partly false. Per Wikipedia/HaMichlol he DID begin serving as admor in 1933 (תרצ\"ג) after his uncle's death, then withdrew amid a succession dispute in 1935 and emigrated. Correct framing: he served briefly, then renounced the admorut and declined to resume it in Tel Aviv. 3) 1981 SPLIT: Inaccurate. Both resulting courts are based in JERUSALEM, not \"Bnei Brak vs Jerusalem.\" The smaller breakaway is named \"Slanim\" (סלאנים), led by Rabbi Avraham Weinberg IV; the larger is led by Rabbi Shmuel/Shalom Noach Berezovsky. The trigger was the death of Avraham Weinberg III (June 1981) and the coronation of his son-in-law Berezovsky, which part of the hasidut rejected. The JSON's \"Slonim (Jerusalem) vs Slonim-Weinberg (Bnei Brak)\" mislabels the branch name and location. 4) NAME-CONFUSION: Flag that \"Avraham Yehoshua Heschel\" was ALSO the name of the Baranovich-branch rebbe (Avraham Weinberg II, d.1933, the subject's uncle). The Tel Aviv subject is a DIFFERENT, younger man (1898-1978), son of Yisroel/Yissachar Leib Weinberg of the Slonim-proper branch. The dynastyBackground should make this distinction explicit to avoid conflation.",
        "founderCheck": "REAL and correctly tied to this address. Rabbi Avraham Yehoshua Heschel Weinberg (1898-1978), son of Yisroel/Yissachar Leib Weinberg (3rd Admor of Slonim, Slonim-proper branch), is confirmed by Hebrew Wikipedia and HaMichlol. He personally settled on Tiyomkin St in Tel Aviv and opened a small beit midrash beside his home where Slonim hasidim prayed — this is a genuine person-specific tie to the location, not merely a dynasty present elsewhere. Glatter independently lists him (rebbe-home, Slonim, 1935), matching name, origin, and year.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "DEFENSIBLE. 1935 (תרצ\"ה) is his documented immigration year and the year he withdrew from the admorut; it matches Glatter exactly (year 1935, rebbe-home). The small beit midrash near his home dates to after his settling on Tiyomkin, so anchoring the institution at ~1935 is reasonable. No anachronism.",
        "dynastyCheck": "HOLDS, with caveats. Slonim attribution is correct: founded 1858 in Slonim, Belarus by Avraham Weinberg I (\"Yesod HaAvoda\"), continuing the Kobrin-Lechovitch chain to the Maggid of Mezritch; succession to Shmuel Weinberg (\"Divrei Shmuel\"); split into Slonim (Yisroel Leib) and Baranovich branches; last European rebbe Shlomo David Yehoshua Weinberg killed at Koldichevo Nov 1943; revival in Eretz Israel by Shalom Noach Berezovsky (\"Netivot Shalom\") who founded Yeshivat Beit Avraham in Jerusalem (~1941) — all confirmed. CAVEATS: (a) the 1858 founding date is from a son/text named \"Yesod HaAvoda\"; sources vary on exact founding year but 1858 is the standard figure and acceptable. (b) the 1981-split description is inaccurate (see corrections). (c) name-confusion between two \"Avraham Yehoshua Heschel\" Weinbergs is glossed.",
        "imageCheck": "N/A — no images claimed. images:[] is empty, so there is no fabricated or wrongly-licensed image to flag. The JSON correctly notes the only Wikipedia image is a gravestone (excluded by rules) and that no free-licensed portrait was found. This is honest and compliant; no portrait was invented.",
