      "verdict": {
        "overallVerdict": "mostly-solid",
        "addressCheck": "NO — neither Glatter nor any open source supports a Slonim (or any Hasidic) institution at \"Nachalat Binyamin 9.\" Glatter's Slonim entry lists ONLY: dynasty Slonim, rebbe Avraham Yehoshua Heschel Weinberg, origin Slonim, year 1935, type rebbe-home — with NO street and NO house number. So Glatter does not override anything here; it is silent on address, which means it actively fails to support \"Nachalat Binyamin 9.\" The real documented Nachalat Binyamin synagogue is #23 (Chabad/Bobruisk), not #9 and not Slonim. The research correctly flags this as a triple mismatch (wrong number, wrong dynasty, wrong location for Slonim). The \"9\" appears fabricated/erroneous; the true Slonim presence was R. Weinberg's small beit midrash on Tiomkin St. and the Berezovsky-led shtibel (later Bnei Brak, Donolo St.).",
        "sourceQuality": "Good and all-verifiable. All 5 cited URLs resolve and substantively support the research's claims. Hebrew Wikipedia (Weinberg): confirms 1935 aliyah, settled on Tiomkin St., opened a small beit midrash, refused the rebistve — no Nachalat Binyamin. Hebrew Wikipedia (Slonim): confirms 1930s Slonim shtibel under Nachum Zeev Berezovsky, later move to Bnei Brak, 1858 founding, 1981 split — no Nachalat Binyamin. Chabadpedia (Nachalat Binyamin): confirms #23, Bobruisk/Russia chasidim, prior Montefiore 36 site, move in ת\"ש=1940, demolished תשע\"ט=2019. HaMichlol (Berezovsky): confirms he led the TA Slonim shtibel, immigrated 1935 with younger brother Shalom Noach (Netivot Shalom), later Bnei Brak/Donolo St. Sources are encyclopedic/community wikis (not peer-reviewed) but mutually consistent and adequate for these factual claims.",
        "corrections": "1) Address: There is no evidence of any Hasidic institution at \"Nachalat Binyamin 9.\" Correct the map entry — either delete it or relabel. The historic Nachalat Binyamin synagogue is at #23 and is CHABAD (founded by Bobruisk chasidim from Russia, immigrated c.1934-37, first at Montefiore 36, moved to N.B. 23 in 1940/ת\"ש, demolished 2019), NOT Slonim. 2) Dynasty: Slonim attribution at this address is wrong; mapErrorFlag \"wrong-dynasty\" is justified (and arguably also \"wrong-number\"). 3) Slonim's true TA footprint: R. Avraham Yehoshua Heschel Weinberg's small beit midrash on Tiomkin St., plus the shtibel led by R. Nachum Zeev Berezovsky; community later relocated to Bnei Brak (Donolo St.). 4) Founder field: correct that no founder ties to N.B. 9; Weinberg ties to Tiomkin, Berezovsky to the shtibel. 5) Minor data-hygiene note: one WebFetch pass misread the Chabadpedia move-year as 1945; the source text is ת\"ש = 1940, matching the research. 6) Confidence \"low\" is appropriate. No image was attached, so no portrait could be verified — consistent with the research's own note; do not add an unverified image.",
        "dynastyCheck": "Slonim is a real dynasty, accurately described: founded 1858 (תרי\"ח) after R. Moshe of Kobrin's death, by R. Avraham Weinberg I (1803/4-1883), author of \"Yesod Ha'avodah\"; Weinberg dynasty led until WWII; revived in Eretz Israel by R. Shalom Noach Berezovsky (\"Netivot Shalom\"); split 1981 into Slonim (Berezovsky) and Slanim (Weinberg). All confirmed. BUT the dynasty does NOT attach to Nachalat Binyamin 9 — that address (the #23 building) is Chabad/Bobruisk. Dynasty attribution for the map pin is INCORRECT.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "Year 1935 (תרצ\"ה) is defensible as the Slonim aliyah year — both R. Weinberg and the Berezovsky brothers immigrated in 1935 (confirmed by Wikipedia and HaMichlol). The research correctly distinguishes this from the Chabad/Bobruisk Nachalat Binyamin 23 synagogue, which was occupied in 1940 (ת\"ש). No anachronism. Note: 1935 dates the Slonim PEOPLE, not a \"Nachalat Binyamin 9\" institution, which has no documented founding date because it likely never existed.",
        "founderCheck": "Founders are real people, correctly identified: R. Avraham Yehoshua Heschel Weinberg (Slonim rebbe, settled Tiomkin St., opened small beit midrash, refused to serve as rebbe) and R. Nachum Zeev Berezovsky (1904-1969, led TA Slonim shtibel, older brother of R. Shalom Noach Berezovsky, immigrated 1935, later Bnei Brak/Donolo St.). Both verified. CRITICAL: neither is tied to \"Nachalat Binyamin 9.\" Glatter names Weinberg for Slonim but gives no address. The Nachalat Binyamin 23 building's founders were Bobruisk/Chabad chasidim — a different group entirely. So founder-to-address linkage for the map pin fails.",
        "imageCheck": "No image was attached in the research JSON, and the research itself notes no free-licensed portrait was verified. Nothing to validate; correctly no fabricated image URL or gravestone was supplied. Do not add an unverified portrait.",
        "flaggedClaims": [
          "Address 'נחלת בנימין 9' — unsupported/likely fabricated; no source documents any institution at #9. The real building is #23.",
