      "verdict": {
        "overallVerdict": "mostly-solid",
        "addressCheck": "SPLIT VERDICT — the corrected address is right; the map address is wrong. (1) The map's \"דיזנגוף 222\" is NOT supported by any source: a targeted search of Dizengoff 222 returns only real-estate listings (madlan.co.il) with zero Hasidic/synagogue content. The research's mapErrorFlag=\"wrong-number\" is correct. (2) The corrected address \"דיזנגוף 36\" IS supported, but ONLY by the Glatter index (archival file את\"ב 1870360), which the prompt says may override open-web geographic reasoning. The encyclopedic sources (he.wikipedia, hamichlol, yeshiva.org.il, the tour group writeup) ALL confirm a Modzhitz beit midrash on Dizengoff Street but NONE of them independently give a house number — they only say \"רחוב דיזנגוף.\" So the number 36 rests entirely on Glatter; it is not independently corroborated on the open web, but it is also not contradicted. Glatter wins here over the unsourced map number 222. Net: address corrected to Dizengoff 36 is the best-supported answer; confidence in the specific number is medium and single-sourced.",
        "sourceQuality": "Mixed but mostly real. VERIFIED working & supportive: he.wikipedia \"חסידות מודז'יץ\" and \"שמואל אליהו טאוב\" (both resolve, confirm founder, Lilienblum→Dizengoff move, musical legacy, Bnei Brak relocation); Wikimedia Commons portrait file (resolves, real PD photo). FAILED TO RESOLVE in this session: yeshiva.org.il rebbe page (HTTP 403) and the Glatter BGU PDF (ECONNREFUSED/connection refused) — so the Glatter PDF could NOT be independently opened; its content is taken on trust from the supplied index quote, exactly as the research's own gaps note admits. The Glatter citation is an academic peer-reviewed source (Iyunim 36, Ben-Gurion Institute) and is the linchpin for the house number; its inaccessibility in this check is a real limitation. hamichlol mirror corroborated the encyclopedic narrative as a cross-check.",
        "corrections": "1) FOUNDING-YEAR/FOUNDER CONFLATION (most important): The claim presents the Dizengoff beit midrash as \"founded 1936\" by \"Rabbi Shmuel Eliyahu Taub.\" Chronology check: Shmuel Eliyahu Taub immigrated to Tel Aviv in 1935 but did NOT become the Modzhitz rebbe until 1947/48, when his father (Shaul Yedidya Elazar Taub, the 2nd rebbe, \"Imrei Shaul\") died. So in 1936 he was a rebbe's son, not the rebbe; describing a \"1936 rebbe-home / center of the Modzhitz court led by Shmuel Eliyahu\" is mildly anachronistic. The research partially hedges by co-naming the father as founder, which is acceptable, but the father's own court center was elsewhere — the 1936 Dizengoff date is best read as a property/registration date (per Glatter), not the founding of Shmuel Eliyahu's rebbe-court. Recommend rewording founder/year as: \"property associated with the Taub family from ~1936 (Glatter archival date); became the Tel Aviv center of Modzhitz under R. Shmuel Eliyahu Taub after he assumed the rebbe-ship in 1947/48.\"\n2) WHO MOVED TO BNEI BRAK IN 1995 — FACTUAL ERROR: The story_he/dynastyBackground_he imply R. Shmuel Eliyahu Taub \"established the center in Tel Aviv... and in 1995 the center was moved to Bnei Brak,\" suggesting one continuous leadership. But Shmuel Eliyahu Taub DIED in 1984. The 1995 relocation to Bnei Brak (Chavakuk/חבקוק St., inaugurated Lag BaOmer 1995) was carried out by his SON, R. Israel Dan Taub, the 4th rebbe (\"Nachlat Dan,\" d. 2006). The dynastyBackground sentence \"ואחריו הנהיג רבי שמואל אליהו טאוב... בשנת 1995 הועבר מרכז החסידות לבני ברק\" telescopes two generations and is misleading. Correct it to attribute the 1995 move to Israel Dan Taub.\n3) ORIGIN \"קראטשוב (Kratshov)\": Glatter lists origin as Kratshov; the dynasty's namesake town is Demblin/Modrzyce (Modzhitz), Poland. \"Kratshov\" is at best the rebbe's personal prior locale, not the dynasty origin — do not present it as the dynasty's origin town. Minor.\n4) BNEI BRAK \"yeshiva 1981 vs center 1995\": A Modzhitz yeshiva named for the father was founded in Bnei Brak in 1981 (while Shmuel Eliyahu was still alive); the full court center moved in 1995. The claim conflates these into a single \"1995 move\" — acceptable shorthand but worth distinguishing.\nNON-ERRORS (hold up): founder names real; dynasty attribution to Modzhitz is correct and well-sourced; the Lilienblum→Dizengoff sequence is confirmed by multiple sources; the musical-legacy framing is accurate; the \"melting pot / openness to non-Hasidim including Habima/Mizrahi youth\" is supported; currentStatus \"active\" is plausible (sources say the old Dizengoff shul kept operating after 1995) but is NOT directly verified for 2026 — keep it flagged as unverified.",
        "dynastyCheck": "CORRECT. Modzhitz (מודז'יץ) is a genuine Hasidic dynasty, branch associated with Kozhnitz, founded by R. Israel Taub (\"Divrei Yisrael,\" d. 1921) in Demblin/Modrzyce, Poland, renowned for its niggun/melody tradition. Succession R. Israel → R. Shaul Yedidya Elazar (\"Imrei Shaul,\" d. 1948) → R. Shmuel Eliyahu (\"Imrei Esh,\" d. 1984) → R. Israel Dan (\"Nachlat Dan,\" d. 2006) → R. Chaim Shaul (current) is confirmed by Wikipedia and Hamichlol. The Dizengoff beit midrash is correctly tied to this dynasty.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "SHAKY / single-sourced and mildly anachronistic. \"1936\" comes only from Glatter (as the rebbe-home/property year). Encyclopedic sources do not give a year for the Dizengoff beit midrash specifically; they establish that Shmuel Eliyahu settled in Tel Aviv in 1935 and became rebbe only in 1947/48. So 1936 is defensible as a property/early-residence date but should NOT be presented as the year R. Shmuel Eliyahu founded his rebbe-court there, since he was not yet rebbe. Defensible with caveat, not clean.",
        "founderCheck": "REAL but with a leadership-timeline error. R. Shmuel Eliyahu Taub (1905–1984), 3rd Modzhitz rebbe, is a real historical figure tied to the Tel Aviv Modzhitz beit midrash — correct. His father R. Shaul Yedidya Elazar Taub (2nd rebbe) is also real and correctly co-named. BUT the research narrative wrongly keeps Shmuel Eliyahu as the leader through the 1995 Bnei Brak relocation; that relocation was his son Israel Dan Taub's act, 11 years after Shmuel Eliyahu's death. Founder identity = correct; founder-as-of-1995 = wrong.",
        "imageCheck": "VERIFIED GOOD. File:Shmuel_Eliyahu_Taub_MODZITZER.jpg exists on Wikimedia Commons, is a genuine photographic PORTRAIT of R. Shmuel Eliyahu Taub (captioned 1905–1984), uploaded 2007, public domain (PD-Poland: published pre-1994 without copyright notice; also PD-US). Not a gravestone, not a fabricated URL. The upload.wikimedia.org direct URL (/f/fc/...) matches the file. License and caption in the claim are accurate.",
        "flaggedClaims": [
          "foundingYear 1936 presented as the founding of R. Shmuel Eliyahu Taub's rebbe-court — anachronistic; he became rebbe only in 1947/48, so 1936 can only be a property/residence date (single-sourced to Glatter)",
          "dynastyBackground/story imply continuous leadership by Shmuel Eliyahu Taub through the 1995 Bnei Brak move — FALSE; he died 1984, the 1995 move was by his son Israel Dan Taub (4th rebbe)",
