        "addressCheck": "PARTIALLY SUPPORTED — with an important caveat. The address \"שדרות נורדאו 65, תל אביב\" is confirmed by the GuideStar/Ministry of Justice corporate registry (org #580163335, \"בית הכנסת דחסידי גור, שדרות נורדאו 65\"), by the Calcalist investigation, and by current real-estate listings for the residential project now on the site (yad2, zemour-realty, project-tlv, nordau65.co.il). So the house number 65 IS independently verified — but NOT by Glatter. The claim is candid about this: the Glatter index entry for גור (Gur) gives NO street and NO house number (only archival ref אעת\"א, חטיבה 8, מיכל 2331, תיק 30-1960-2 and the \"16 places of worship\" claim). Therefore Glatter neither confirms nor contradicts \"Nordau 65\"; verification of the number rests on the registry + journalism, which is solid. The claim states this honestly in glatterMatch_he and gaps.",
        "sourceQuality": "Good overall, with one mis-cited URL. Strong sources: (1) GuideStar/Ministry of Justice registry resolves and confirms name + address; (2) Calcalist \"צבא הכסף של גור\" (newmedia.calcalist.co.il/magazine-05-10-23/m01.html) resolves and supports the full sale chain (1-shekel valuation ~2013, 9M sale 2014, 15M resale 2015, 21.6M to Yochananof 2020, loan→8.5M donation in 2018, six-floor residential project on site); (3) he.wikipedia confirms 7 synagogues in 1949 and Gur founder Yitzchak Meir Alter / move to Bnei Brak; (4) en.wikipedia confirms Yisrael Alter as 5th Rebbe 1948–1977, died 1977 Jerusalem, role in founding HaModia; (5) Wikimedia image is real and CC BY-SA 4.0. PROBLEM: one of the cited Calcalist URLs (hy4kl8m00o) is actually about a DIFFERENT Gur synagogue in Ramat Gan (Shilo St 18, sold 9.652M), not Nordau 65 — a conflation risk, though it was not in the claim's source list.",
        "founderCheck": "FAILS / UNSUPPORTED CONFLATION. The claim names the founder/local representative as \"יהודה מאיר אברמוביץ\". He is a real person (1914–2007, Lodz-born MK who represented Gur/Agudat Yisrael in the Knesset and chaired Agudat Yisrael's world executive), but NO source ties him to founding, endowing, or even being the local representative of THIS synagogue at Nordau 65. The actual documented owner/endower per Calcalist is \"הקדש מאיר חנוך הכהן ורשבסקי ואשתו רבקה\" (the hekdesh/endowment of Meir Chanoch HaKohen Warshawski and his wife Rivka), who owned the entire six-apartment building, one apartment of which was the synagogue. The \"אברמוביץ\" attribution appears to be drawn from the Glatter index's note that Abramowitz was the local Gur representative in Tel Aviv generally — not for this address. This is an unsupported conflation of a political/communal figure with a building-specific founder.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "DEFENSIBLE AS STATED (i.e., honestly marked \"unknown\"). The claim does not assert a specific founding year for the Nordau 65 building and explicitly flags it as unknown, attributing only the broader community timeline (1920s–30s establishment, ~16 shtiblach by early תש\"ך/1960). That is consistent with sources. No anachronism introduced. The real, verifiable framing would be the Warshawski hekdesh, whose exact dating I could not establish on the open web.",
        "dynastyCheck": "HOLDS. Gur (גור / Ger) dynasty attribution is correct and well-supported. Glatter lists גור (Gur) with type beit-midrash, rebbe Yisrael Alter (Jerusalem-based, outside city) and local rep Abramowitz, and the \"16 places of worship in early 1960\" figure. Wikipedia confirms Gur as a major Polish dynasty founded by Yitzchak Meir Alter (Chiddushei HaRim), led post-Holocaust by Yisrael Alter (Beis Yisroel, 1948–1977), and operating 7 synagogues in Tel Aviv in 1949 before relocating its center to Bnei Brak. All dynasty-background details check out. The claim correctly does NOT attribute central-court (Bait Yisrael) events to this local address.",
        "imageCheck": "REAL and FREE-LICENSED, but NOT a clean solo portrait — minor caveats. File:Beis_Yisroel.jpg on Wikimedia Commons is a genuine image, CC BY-SA 4.0, genuinely depicting Rabbi Yisrael Alter (Beis Yisroel of Gur). NOT a gravestone, NOT a fabricated URL — it resolves. However: (1) the image also includes two other figures (Rabbi Yaakov Weil and the Tolna Rebbe), so the caption \"portrait of Rabbi Yisrael Alter\" is slightly imprecise — he is one of three people shown. (2) The \"date\" 2007 (3 Oct 2007) is an upload/file-metadata artifact, NOT the photo date — Yisrael Alter died in 1977, so the photo necessarily predates that. The claim's gaps section already acknowledges both issues correctly.",
