        "overallVerdict": "shaky",
        "addressCheck": "NOT SUPPORTED. Neither Glatter nor any open-web source places a Gur institution at \"גוש חלב 28\". The Glatter index entry for Gur has NO street and NO number field at all — it carries only the archival ref (אעת\"א, חטיבה 8, מיכל 2331, תיק 30-1960-2) and the \"16 batei tfila by early תש\"ך/1960\" quote. So Glatter explicitly does NOT support Gush Halav 28 or any house number for Gur; the research honestly admits this. On the open web: the official Tel Aviv Religious Council (rabanut.co.il) and Kipa list the active Gur beit midrash at פיירברג (Feierberg) 17, and a separate Gur \"שטיבלאך גור\" at הדף היומי (Daf Hayomi) 8 — two real Gur addresses, NEITHER of which is Gush Halav. project-tlv.info shows Gush Halav street has documented buildings only at numbers 9 and 10 and no religious institution; no #28 is documented. The map address is unverifiable and likely a conflation/error.",
        "sourceQuality": "Mixed but honest. Cited rabanut.co.il and Kipa pages are real and resolve (Kipa returned HTTP 403 to the fetcher but is confirmed live via search and corroborates Feierberg 17). tlv-streets/yonbergman and project-tlv.info resolve and actually support the NEGATIVE claim (no religious institution on Gush Halav street). Hebrew Wikipedia for the Gur dynasty and Israel Alter is reliable. The Glatter archival reference is internally consistent. The sources genuinely establish that there is NO Gur presence at Gush Halav — so the sourcing backs the skepticism, not the positive map address. No fabricated URLs detected among those tested.",
        "dynastyCheck": "Dynasty attribution (Gur / חסידות גור) is correct and well-documented as the largest Hasidic group in mid-20th-century Tel Aviv. BUT a factual error: research calls Israel Alter \"האדמו\"ר הרביעי בשושלת\" (fourth Rebbe). He was the FIFTH. Hebrew Wikipedia order: (1) Yitzhak Meir Alter [Chidushei HaRim], (2) Chanoch Henich ha-Kohen Levin of Alexander, (3) Yehudah Aryeh Leib Alter [Sfat Emet], (4) Avraham Mordechai Alter [Imrei Emet], (5) Israel Alter [Beit Yisrael]. The research's own dynastyBackground narrative omits the 2nd Rebbe (Chanoch Henich Levin), which is the source of the off-by-one error.",
        "founderCheck": "Founder figures are real. Israel Alter (1894–1977), the \"Beit Yisrael,\" became Rebbe in 1948 (after his father's death on Shavuot 5708) and lived in Jerusalem — all confirmed. He is NOT a founder of this specific site; he is the dynasty leader who sat OUTSIDE Tel Aviv (Jerusalem), so he was never \"tied to this address.\" The dynasty founder is correctly given as Yitzhak Meir Alter (1859, Góra Kalwaria). No founder is specifically attested for any Gur site at Gush Halav 28 — none exists in the record. The local representative named (יהודה מאיר אברמוביץ) matches Glatter.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "No founding year is claimed for this site (research lists \"unknown\"), which is defensible — Glatter gives no year for Gur, only that Gur reached ~16 prayer houses by early תש\"ך/1960. The \"1949, ~7 Gur synagogues under Rabbi Shaul Moshe Silberman\" detail in the background is not in the Glatter index and was not verifiable in the sources tested; treat as unconfirmed. No anachronism in the year handling itself.",
        "imageCheck": "No images claimed (images array empty). Research correctly notes no free-licensed portrait of Israel Alter exists because he avoided being photographed. No fabricated image URLs to flag. Acceptable.",
        "flaggedClaims": [
          "DYNASTY-COUNT ERROR: Israel Alter called 'fourth Rebbe' — he was the FIFTH (the background omits Chanoch Henich ha-Kohen Levin of Alexander as 2nd Rebbe).",
          "ADDRESS UNSUPPORTED: 'גוש חלב 28' is corroborated by NO source — not Glatter (no street/number for Gur), not the Religious Council (Feierberg 17), not Kipa (also lists Daf Hayomi 8), not the street pages (no religious institution; buildings only at #9, #10).",
          "GEOGRAPHIC MISLABEL: research places Gush Halav street in 'הצפון הישן' (old north). Street coordinates ~32.0855, 34.7752 fall in central Tel Aviv (Lev Ha'ir, west of Rothschild), not the old north — the internal geographic reasoning is itself wrong.",
          "CONFLATION RISK: The real documented Gur sites (Feierberg 17 beit midrash; Daf Hayomi 8 shtiblach) are conflated/displaced onto an unrelated street; the map entry appears to be a misplacement.",
          "UNVERIFIED DETAIL: '~7 Gur synagogues in 1949 under R. Shaul Moshe Silberman' not found in Glatter or tested web sources."
        ],
        "corrections": "1) Change 'האדמו\"ר הרביעי בשושלת' to 'החמישי' and add Chanoch Henich ha-Kohen Levin of Alexander as the 2nd Rebbe in the dynasty sequence. 2) Mark the address as a likely map error: there is NO evidence of any Gur (or any) synagogue at Gush Halav 28. The documented active Gur sites in Tel Aviv are Feierberg 17 (beit midrash, per the official Religious Council) and Daf Hayomi 8 ('שטיבלאך גור', per Kipa). If the map intended the real Gur institution, the correctedAddress should be Feierberg 17 (or Daf Hayomi 8 for the shtiblach), not Gush Halav 28. 3) Fix the neighborhood: Gush Halav street is in central Tel Aviv, not the 'old north'. 4) Glatter is correct over the research here — Glatter assigns NO house number to Gur (only a count of ~16 prayer houses and the Jerusalem-based Rebbe with local rep Yehuda Meir Abramowitz); the open-web Feierberg 17 / Daf Hayomi 8 addresses come from current directories, not Glatter. Where they 'disagree', neither supports Gush Halav 28, so the map address should be flagged as fabricated/unverifiable. 5) Remove or caveat the unverified 1949/Silberman detail. Overall: the dynasty, founders, and historical significance are sound; the SPECIFIC site address is fabricated/unsupported and one dynasty-count fact is wrong."
