      "verdict": {
        "overallVerdict": "mostly-solid",
        "addressCheck": "PARTIALLY SUPPORTED, but NOT by Glatter. The address \"שינקין 25, תל אביב\" is supported only by open-web Hebrew sources: Hebrew Wikipedia \"חסידות קוז'ניץ\" states verbatim \"תחת כהונתו נוסדו שני בתי מדרש בתל אביב (שינקין 25) ובירושלים (חפץ חיים 34)\". That is a real, resolving source that explicitly supports the house number. HOWEVER, the Glatter index does NOT support Sheinkin 25, does NOT list Grodzisk/Piaseczno under any street or house number, and carries NO archival file number (את\"ב/אעת\"א) for this institution — its single relevant record is type \"rebbe-home\" with origin \"תל אביב\", year 1946, and no street/number. So Glatter neither confirms nor refutes the street; it only confirms the man + Tel Aviv + 1946. The ynet \"כשרחוב שינקין היה חרדי\" source, cited as corroboration, does NOT mention Grodzisk, Sheinkin 25, or Shapira at all (it covers Belz/Gur/Ruzhin/Sadigura) — it is only atmospheric, not probative. No independent archival document ties this specific address to this rebbe; confidence \"medium\" is appropriate, arguably generous.",
        "sourceQuality": "Mixed. The two Wikipedia/Michlol articles on Avraham Elimelech Shapira and the קוז'ניץ article are real, resolve, and directly support the man, dynasty, 1946 Admorut, electrician backstory, 1928 aliyah, Kfar Hasidim, and BOTH batei midrash addresses. These are tertiary encyclopedic sources (Wikipedia/Michlol), not archival. The ynet citation is essentially a decorative/non-supporting source — it does not back the institution. Glatter is authoritative for what it asserts (man + Tel Aviv + 1946) but provides no archival ref for THIS site, unlike e.g. Modzhitz/Vizhnitz/Bohush entries which carry real file numbers. So address rests on a single encyclopedic source family, not on Glatter's archival apparatus.",
        "founderCheck": "CONFLATED / MISLABELED. The top-level \"founder\" field — 'רבי אברהם אלימלך שפירא, האדמו\"ר מגרודז'יסק' — is wrong if read as founder of the DYNASTY: he was a late-generation rebbe (great-grandson line), not the dynasty's founder. The dynasty's actual founder was רבי אלימלך שפירא מגרודז'יסק (1824–1892), as the research's own dynastyBackground correctly states — so the JSON contradicts itself between the \"founder\" field and \"dynastyBackground\". Charitably, \"founder\" here means founder of the Tel Aviv institution, which is defensible. As the leader who established the Sheinkin 25 beit midrash, Avraham Elimelech Shapira is correctly identified and is a real historical figure (confirmed by Wikipedia, Michlol, and Glatter). Glatter lists him by name under \"פיאסצנה\".",
        "foundingYearCheck": "DEFENSIBLE. \"After 1946 (תש\"ו)\" is consistent across all sources: Hebrew Wikipedia/Michlol date his acceptance of the Admorut to 1946 after learning of his family's deaths in the Holocaust, and Glatter independently states \"1946; קיבל עליו את האדמו\"רות\". The beit midrash on Sheinkin is tied to his tenure (\"תחת כהונתו\"), which began 1946, so a post-1946 founding is sound. Minor note: aliyah year varies between sources (1928 per Wikipedia/Michlol vs 1926 in one web summary); the 1946 Admorut year is robust.",
        "dynastyCheck": "SUPPORTED with a caveat. Grodzisk is a genuine offshoot of the Koznitz (Maggid of Kozhnitz) line, and the Grodzisk–Piaseczno kinship (both descend from founder Elimelech Shapira of Grodzisk) is real, so the \"גרודז'יסק-פיאסצנה\" label and Koznitz parentage are legitimate. Avraham Elimelech was indeed the Grodzisk rebbe (per Wikipedia/Michlol). The friction is purely taxonomic: Glatter files the SAME man under \"פיאסצנה\", while the research files him under \"גרודז'יסק\". Both are defensible because the courts are intertwined and he carried Grodzisk leadership; the research is actually MORE precise here than Glatter, since his primary title was Grodzisk (Piaseczno passed to the Israeli line via a different branch). Neither is \"wrong\"; they are two valid labels for one intertwined family.",
        "imageCheck": "No image asserted — images array is empty, and gaps correctly explains that no free-licensed portrait was found on Wikimedia Commons (the only hit was an unrelated Petah Tikva building). This is the honest/correct outcome; no fabricated or mislabeled image to flag. Good restraint.",
