      "verdict": {
        "overallVerdict": "mostly-solid",
        "addressCheck": "Glatter neither supports nor contradicts the house number. Glatter's only Chabad entry is type \"shtibel\" with NO street, NO number, and NO archival file. Its single quote is the generic statement that Chabad \"did not put down deep roots in Tel Aviv\" and moved its center to Kfar Chabad under R. Yosef Yitzchak Schneersohn. So the address Nachalat Binyamin 23 rests ENTIRELY on open-web sources (Chabadpedia and the Tel Aviv Religious Council / rabanut.co.il), both of which I confirmed do state Nachalat Binyamin 23. The research is honest about this: no Glatter override exists and there is no archival file number for this site. correctedAddress correctly left empty; mapErrorFlag \"none\" is appropriate. Caveat: the house number has only open-web (largely Chabad-internal/wiki) confirmation, not an independent archival source like Glatter provides for Modzhitz, Vizhnitz, Boyan, etc.",
        "sourceQuality": "Mixed but adequate. Chabadpedia is a community wiki (self-published, Chabad-internal); it directly and fully supports the narrative (Montefiore 36 start, move to Nachalat Binyamin 23 in 1940/tash\"sh, Zevin then Karasik, 2019 demolition, temporary relocation to Yavneh St), but is not independent scholarship. rabanut.co.il (Tel Aviv Religious Council) is an independent official listing that corroborates address and dynasty. The Yosef Yitzchak Schneersohn Wikipedia page and the Wikimedia Commons image page both resolve and support their claims. Zevin is independently corroborated across Hebrew Wikipedia, Hamichlol, and emess.co.il. No primary/archival source backs the exact house number — the main quality gap. All four cited URLs resolve and genuinely support what they are cited for.",
