        "founderCheck": "Largely supported, one nuance. The community founders (\"Chabad chassidim from Russia in Tel Aviv\") plus first rabbi R. Shlomo Yosef Zevin, succeeded by R. Chaim Eliezer Karasik — confirmed by Chabadpedia AND independently by Hebrew Wikipedia, Hamichlol, and emess.co.il. NOTE: the research's own gaps section understates this, claiming Zevin \"appears in one secondary source and was not verified in multiple independent sources\" — that is overly cautious; Zevin is multiply attested. Karasik's full name is correctly given as Chaim Eliezer (chabad.info obituary confirms \"הרב חיים אליעזר קרסיק\"); the \"Eliezer Karasik\" form seen on Hamichlol/rabanut is just a shortened form, not a contradiction. The founder is real and specifically tied to THIS congregation, not merely the dynasty elsewhere.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "Defensible but needs framing care. The \"founding year\" is genuinely two dates and the claim states both: the community began on Montefiore 36 in the 1920s, and moved to Nachalat Binyamin 23 in 1940 (tash\"sh). Both are stated by Chabadpedia. Glatter gives no year for Chabad at all, so no conflict. Minor chronology tension worth flagging: chabad.info says Karasik died in 1940 (5 Nisan tash\"sh) after serving \"nearly half a yovel\" (~25 yrs), which is consistent with the move year but means his tenure on Nachalat Binyamin itself would have been at most months — the \"served over 20 years\" figure refers to his whole communal tenure, not service at the new building. Not an error, just a subtlety.",
        "imageCheck": "PASS. File:Frierdiger_Rebbe.tif is a REAL, free-licensed (CC BY-SA 3.0 Unported, OTRS/VRT ticket confirmed) PORTRAIT photograph of R. Yosef Yitzchak Schneersohn (the 6th Rebbe), sourced from Jewish Educational Media, photo by Barry Gurary, early 1920s. It is a genuine portrait, not a gravestone, not a fabricated URL — the URL resolves. The caption is HONEST: it explicitly identifies him as the dynasty's serving Rebbe during the synagogue's activity, NOT as the local synagogue rabbi, and the gaps section repeats this disclaimer. Acceptable use.",
