        "gaps": "סתירה בשנת היסוד: גלאטר מתעד 1936 (קשור לעליית האב שאול ידידיה ב-1935 ולרישום הארכיוני את\"ב תיק 1870360), בעוד שמקורות אינטרנט (ויקישיבה) מזכירים 1958 כשנת הקמת בית המדרש הספציפי בדיזנגוף 36 בידי רבי שמואל אליהו טאוב; ייתכן שמדובר בהתפתחות הדרגתית - תחילה מרכז עם עליית האב ובהמשך בניית בית המדרש הגדול. בהתאם להנחיות מועדפת שנת גלאטר (1936). יחס המשפחתי: גלאטר מנסח \"ואביו שאול ידידיה טאוב\", בעוד שלפי ויקיפדיה שמואל אליהו היה למעשה נכדו (לא בנו) של מייסד החצר ישראל טאוב, ובן/יורש של שאול ידידיה. הקשר בין שמואל אליהו לשאול ידידיה: ויקיפדיה מציינת יחסי אב-בן, אך יש גרסאות שונות לגבי הקרבה המדויקת. דיוקן הרבי הוא נחלת הכלל ואומת ישירות בוויקישיתוף."
      },
      "verdict": {
        "overallVerdict": "mostly-solid",
        "addressCheck": "SUPPORTED. Dizengoff 36 is corroborated independently by (a) the Tel Aviv Religious Council (rabanut.co.il) listing \"תפארת שאול - חסידי מודזיץ\" at \"דיזינגוף 36, לב העיר\", nusach Ashkenaz; and (b) the Glatter article, whose Modzhitz entry gives רחוב דיזנגוף, number 36, registered to the עמותה \"בית מודז'יץ\". Glatter and the open web AGREE on the house number — no correction needed (correctedAddress correctly empty, mapErrorFlag none). A Google snippet of Glatter adds that the property was registered to \"בית מודז'יץ\" headed by Rivka Zelta Taub, wife of R. Shmuel Eliyahu, consistent with archival registration. The synagogue is currently active at the address.",
        "sourceQuality": "Mixed but mostly solid. Glatter (Iyunim be-Tkumat Yisrael, Ben-Gurion University) is a genuine peer-reviewed source, and a Google search confirms it covers Modzhitz at Dizengoff 36 under R. Shmuel Eliyahu Taub. CAVEAT: I could not directly open the PDF (in.bgu.ac.il returned ECONNREFUSED on every attempt, http and https), so the exact archival file numbers (תיק 1870360; docs 190260/190293/190295/190569/190567) and the \"1936\" year string could not be byte-verified against the primary text — they rest on the index alone, and 1936 is historically implausible. he.wikipedia (Shmuel Eliyahu Taub; Modzhitz; Shaul Yedidya Taub), hamichlol, and rabanut.co.il all resolve and corroborate the people, lineage and address. The Wikimedia Commons image page resolves and is a genuine free-licensed portrait. niguney-modzitz/machon-modzitz are non-scholarly fan sites but consistent.",
        "founderCheck": "Founders are REAL and correctly named but mis-sequenced. Dynasty founder: R. Yisrael Taub (Divrei Yisrael), Modzhitz est. 1889. The claim's \"founder\" R. Shaul Yedidya Elazar Taub (Imrei Shaul, 1886-1947) is real but did NOT found the Dizengoff institution: he lived in NY ~1940-47 and made aliyah only in 1947, dying months later (15 Kislev תש\"ח = 29 Nov 1947, buried on the Mount of Olives). The actual establisher of the Dizengoff 36 beit midrash is R. Shmuel Eliyahu Taub (Imrei Esh, 1905-1984), 3rd rebbe, SON of Shaul Yedidya and GRANDSON of Yisrael. Glatter ties Shmuel Eliyahu specifically to Dizengoff 36, so the founder IS tied to THIS address — but the claim wrongly credits the FATHER with founding a 1930s Tel Aviv center.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "NOT defensible as stated. \"1936 (Glatter)\" is historically implausible: the father was abroad (Poland, then NY until 1947), and the son became rebbe only in 1947; the Dizengoff hall is dated 1958 in open sources. The 1935 date in the narrative belongs to a family VISIT (the son accompanying his father to EY in תרצ\"ה), not an institutional founding. Treat 1936 as a likely Glatter error; ~1958 under Shmuel Eliyahu is the defensible date. The gap note's instruction to \"prefer Glatter's 1936\" propagates the error.",
        "dynastyCheck": "Dynasty attribution HOLDS: Modzhitz (מודז'יץ), the Taub line, \"ממלכת הניגון,\" matches both Glatter and Wikipedia; main court today in Bnei Brak — confirmed. ONLY the origin TOWN is wrong: it is Demblin/Dęblin (called by Jews \"Modzhitz\"), NOT \"קראטשוב / Kratshov.\" Where Glatter and the web disagree on origin, the WEB is correct.",
        "imageCheck": "VALID. File:Shmuel_Eliyahu_Taub_MODZITZER.jpg on Wikimedia Commons resolves to a genuine PORTRAIT photograph (365x530px) of Rebbe Shmuel Eliyahu Taub of Modzitz (1905-1984), 3rd Rebbe, sourced from modzitz.org, uploaded 2007. NOT a gravestone, not a fabricated URL. Public domain (pre-1989 foreign publication without notice; Polish law). Caption correctly identifies the subject and ties him to Dizengoff 36.",
        "flaggedClaims": [
          "CONFLATION/FABRICATION: 'רבי שאול ידידיה אלעזר טאוב עלה לארץ ישראל ב-1935 והקים מרכז בתל אביב' — he actually fled WWII via Russia/Japan to the US (NY beit midrash ~1940-41) and made aliyah only in 1947, dying that same year; the 1935 event was his son's accompanying VISIT, not a Tel Aviv founding.",
          "ANACHRONISM: foundingYear 1936 for a Modzhitz rebbe-home at Dizengoff 36 — neither rebbe led a TA institution in 1936; the son became rebbe in 1947 and the hall is dated 1958 in open sources.",
          "DUBIOUS ORIGIN: origin town 'קראטשוב / Kratshov' contradicts the documented dynastic seat Demblin/Dęblin (Modzhitz).",
          "UNVERIFIED PRIMARY CITATIONS: archival file 1870360 and document numbers (190260/190293/190295/190569/190567) could not be confirmed against the Glatter PDF, which would not load (ECONNREFUSED); accepted only on the index's word.",
          "MUDDLED LINEAGE WORDING: 'בנו (לפי המקורות הפתוחים נכדו)' — Shmuel Eliyahu is SON of Shaul Yedidya and GRANDSON of dynasty-founder Yisrael Taub; the phrasing conflates the two ancestors.",
          "OVERSTATEMENT: significance_he implies the Dizengoff hall was the Modzhitz court's EY center during BOTH Shaul Yedidya's and Shmuel Eliyahu's eras — Shaul Yedidya's brief 1947 EY period (and Mount of Olives burial) does not support a Dizengoff-centered tenure for him."
        ],
        "corrections": "1) FOUNDER/ALIYAH CONFLATION (most serious): Shaul Yedidya did NOT come in 1935 to found a Tel Aviv center. He fled via Russia→Japan→America, ran a NY beit midrash ~1940-41, made permanent aliyah only in 1947 and died 15 Kislev תש\"ח (29 Nov 1947), buried on the Mount of Olives. The 1935 (תרצ\"ה) event was his SON Shmuel Eliyahu accompanying him on a VISIT. Rewrite the narrative so the Dizengoff 36 beit midrash is attributed to the THIRD rebbe, Shmuel Eliyahu Taub (1905-1984), who inherited the admorut in 1947, first prayed on Lilienblum, then moved to Dizengoff.\n2) FOUNDING YEAR: Replace \"1936 (per Glatter)\" — implausible — with \"~1950s (open sources: 1958) under R. Shmuel Eliyahu Taub.\" Flag Glatter's 1936 as a probable error/conflation with the dynasty's earlier presence rather than a Dizengoff-36 founding.\n3) ORIGIN TOWN: Change \"Kratshov (קראטשוב)\" to Demblin/Dęblin (דמבלין), the town Jews called Modzhitz; dynasty founded by R. Yisrael Taub in 1889. Note that \"Kratshov\" appears only in Glatter and is unsupported by mainstream sources.\n4) LINEAGE: State plainly — Shmuel Eliyahu (Imrei Esh) is the SON of Shaul Yedidya (Imrei Shaul) and the GRANDSON of dynasty-founder R. Yisrael Taub (Divrei Yisrael). Glatter's \"ואביו שאול ידידיה טאוב\" (father Shaul Yedidya) is genealogically accurate. Clean the confusing \"בנו... נכדו\" clause in story_he.\n5) SOURCING: Because the Glatter PDF would not load, mark the archival file/document numbers as index-asserted-but-unverified rather than independently confirmed.\nNet: WHO (Shmuel Eliyahu Taub / Modzhitz), WHERE (Dizengoff 36), the dynasty, the active synagogue (Tiferet Shaul, nusach Ashkenaz), and the image are all real and supported; the WHEN (1936) and the father's-1935-Tel-Aviv-center story are wrong, and origin \"Kratshov\" is dubious."
      }
