        "addressCheck": "NO — Glatter does NOT support the address or house number. Glatter's only Chiddushei HaRim entry is a bare yeshiva record: {dynasty:\"גור (Gur) — ישיבת חידושי הרי\\\"ם\", type:\"yeshiva\", quote:\"כך לדוגמה ישיבת חידושי הרי\\\"ם של חסידי גור\"} — with NO street, NO number, and NO archival file reference. So Glatter cannot confirm or override the address; it only confirms the institution exists and belongs to Gur. The corrected address (street \"פרץ\", number 12) comes solely from open web. He.wikipedia explicitly states: \"בשנת ה'תרצ\\\"ט עברה למבנה שכור בן שלושה חדרים ברחוב פרץ 12\" — so house number 12 (not 13) IS supported by Wikipedia/HaMichlol, and the map's \"13\" is wrong. The map STREET name \"פרץ אוניקובסקי\" is unsupported by any source and appears fabricated/conflated; the actual street is just \"פרץ\". The research's refinement to \"י.ל. פרץ\" is a reasonable INFERENCE (I.L. Peretz St sits in the Florentin/Neve Sha'anan area by the old central station, matching the source's geography) but is NOT literally stated — sources say only \"פרץ\". The map coordinates (32.10707, 34.79463) resolve to Ramat Aviv/Reading in far north TA, which matches neither the historic south-TA site nor the current Ramat HaChayil site, so they are wrong. Current address נמירובר/נמירוב 24, רמת החייל is confirmed by the Tel Aviv Religious Council (Rabanut) page.",
        "sourceQuality": "Mixed but mostly real. He.wikipedia and HaMichlol both resolve and substantively support the founder/year/dynasty/street-12 narrative (they are near-mirrors of each other, so they are NOT two independent sources — effectively one source). The Rabanut (Tel Aviv Religious Council) page resolves and confirms the current address נמירובר 24. The Wikimedia Commons image page resolves and is a genuine PD portrait. The Nominatim reverse-geocode source honestly documents that the map coordinates land in the wrong place (Ramat Aviv), which actually strengthens the research's own error-flagging. The Time Out source is generic street-color about I.L. Peretz St and does NOT tie the yeshiva to that street — it is used only to support the inference. No fabricated source URLs detected; all cited URLs are real domains. Weakness: the entire address/house-number/early-history chain rests on Wikipedia+HaMichlol (one editorial lineage) with NO archival corroboration in Glatter, so confidence \"medium\" is appropriate.",
        "founderCheck": "Partially supported / overstated. Dynasty-level framing holds: he.wiki and HaMichlol both say the yeshiva was \"נפתחה על ידי רבי שמחה בונם אלתר\" under his father the Imrei Emes's direction, so naming Simcha Bunim Alter + Avraham Mordechai Alter is defensible per those sources. BUT adversarial flags: (a) the FIRST rosh yeshiva per Wikipedia was הרב גדליה הרץ (Gedalia Hertz), not Simcha Bunim; (b) Simcha Bunim Alter's OWN Wikipedia bio does NOT credit him as founder and instead says he later \"served as PRESIDENT (נשיא) of the yeshiva\" — i.e. a leadership/figurehead role, post-founding; (c) his bio places him in Eretz Israel from Elul 1934 until 1938, and says that while his father was in Poland he concentrated Gur Hasidim in JERUSALEM (not Tel Aviv). So \"founder\" is the institution's official framing, not a firmly documented personal act of establishment. The Imrei Emes (Avraham Mordechai Alter, 1865–1948) is real and correctly identified as the directing Rebbe.",
        "foundingYearCheck": "Founding year תרצ\"ו (1935/1936) is supported by both he.wiki and HaMichlol. However there is an internal date conflation to flag: the move to \"רחוב פרץ 12\" occurred in תרצ\"ט (1939) per Wikipedia, NOT 1936 — so the address-12 location is a 1939 fact, while 1936 is the founding date (initially in the Gur shtibel on התבור/Tabor St, then the rented Peretz building from 1939). The research's \"permanent building inaugurated 1953\" matches HaMichlol (\"נחנך ב-1953\"). The research's gaps note already half-acknowledges the 1939 figure, which is good. Glatter gives NO year for this yeshiva, so it neither confirms nor refutes.",
        "dynastyCheck": "Correct. Gur (גור / Ger / Góra Kalwaria) is right, founded by R. Yitzchak Meir Alter the \"Chiddushei HaRim\" (whom the yeshiva is named after) — consistent across all sources and Glatter. Glatter independently lists a \"גור (Gur) — ישיבת חידושי הרי\\\"ם\" yeshiva record and a separate Gur beit-midrash record (Rebbe in Jerusalem, local rep Yehuda Meir Abramowicz), matching the research's dynasty background. One nuance the research got right: the Rebbe sat in Jerusalem, not Tel Aviv.",
        "imageCheck": "Valid. File:Imrei_Emes_(cropped).jpg is a genuine portrait photograph of Rabbi Avraham Mordechai Alter (the Imrei Emes), created before 1948, in the Public Domain (copyright expired in Israel/Poland/US). It is a real portrait — NOT a gravestone, NOT a fabricated URL — and the caption correctly identifies him as the Rebbe under whose direction the yeshiva was founded. Appropriate and correctly licensed.",
        "flaggedClaims": [
